@using System.IO;
@using System.Net.Http
@using System.Net.Http.Json
@using System.Text.Json
@using System.Text
@using System.Threading.Tasks
@using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Components.WebAssembly.Http
@using System.Runtime.InteropServices.JavaScript;

@using GdsII;
@using GDSViewer.Models;
@using GDSViewer.Components;

@*
    The app's one route. The view is carried in the query string rather than as a path segment, because
    index.html sets <base href> from window.location.pathname so that publishing to a subdirectory needs
    no rebuild - which means any path below the app root would become the base and break every relative
    asset URL and the import map with it. A query string leaves the path alone.

    It also avoids needing the host to rewrite unknown paths onto index.html, which GitHub Pages does not
    do, so a shared link keeps working wherever this is served from.
*@
@page "/"

@inject HttpClient httpClient
@inject IJSRuntime js;
@inject NavigationManager NavigationManager
@inject AppStorage storage
@inject HistoryStore history
@inject TextureStore textures
@inject PreviewStore previewStore
@inject PdkStore pdkStore
@inject GDSViewer.Ai.IAiService aiService


<PageTitle>GDSII Viewer</PageTitle>


@*
    Every wrapper between the page and the view is a flex column that gives its height away, so the view
    ends up with whatever the window has left after the toolbar and the padding.

    They are all here rather than one rule on the view, because a flex height only travels as far as the
    next element that will not pass it on: one ancestor sized to its own contents and the chain stops
    there. That is what left the app taller than the window and put a scrollbar on the page.
*@
<div class="viewerPage @(fullScreen ? "viewerPageFull" : "")">
    <!-- *********************************************** Main App ***************************************************** -->

    <div class="row viewerPageRow" style="justify-content: center;">
        @*
            data-file names whatever is open. The picker used to be on screen at all times and was the
            answer to "which file is this?"; behind a button it is not, and nothing else on the page says
            so in every view - the layer sidebar can be put away, and the canvas says nothing.
        *@
        <div id="mainAppContainer" class="col" data-file="@(CurrentlySelectedFileName + CurrentlySelectedFileType)">

            @*
                There is no separate top toolbar any more. Everything it held is either in the view's own
                bar below - open, save and share, which act on the file and are reached for constantly - or
                behind the Examples button there, which is where the bundled layouts and the PDK links
                went. One bar rather than two: the second one cost a row of screen to carry three controls,
                and the file picker was the only one of them anybody used twice.
            *@

            <!-- *********************************************** View Change ***************************************************** -->

            @*
                The bar's own padding lives here rather than on each control in it. Every toolbarItem
                carried 10px all round, so the row's height was however tall the tallest control's padding
                made it and the gap above the first item was set by something three components away.
            *@
            @*
                The bar, unless the page asked for the canvas on its own.

                Not merely hidden: left out of the render entirely, so nothing in it is focusable, reachable
                by keyboard, or read out - a toolbar that is invisible and still tabbable is worse than one
                that is there.
            *@
            @if (!viewerOnly)
            {
            <div class="row viewBorderBackground" style="border-bottom: none;">
                 <div class="row viewToolbar">

                     @*
                         What acts on the file, ahead of everything the view contributes. The pair shares
                         one column rather than taking one each: two small icons that read as in and out
                         together, the same way the layer sidebar pairs its own two.
                     *@
                     @*
                         Buttons rather than pictures on the page.

                         They were two PNGs with a cursor over them - the only controls in the bar that were
                         images rather than buttons, and the only ones that did not look pressable. Blue
                         squares with white glyphs, the same as everything else up here, drawn inline so
                         they inherit the color and stay sharp at any zoom.

                         The label keeps its `for`, which is the whole reason a file input is reachable at
                         all: the input itself is hidden and unstyleable, and the label is what stands in
                         for it. Styled as a button, it is one everywhere but in the markup.
                     *@
                     @*
                         No fence after these. The two lists beside them open files too, so the four of them
                         are one group with an ordinary gutter between its halves - the fence belongs past
                         Examples, where the controls stop being about getting a file on screen.
                     *@
                     <div class="col-1 toolbarItem" style="align-self: end; display: flex; gap: 0.4em;">
                         @*
                             The dialog opens on layouts rather than on everything in the folder.

                             Names only - the reader decides what a file is by looking inside it, not by
                             what it is called, so this is the picker's filter and not the app's. A file
                             that arrives some other way is read on its contents exactly as before.

                             .dxf and .svg are in the list because the app opens those too. Leaving them out
                             would hide formats that work from the only dialog most people use to reach them.

                             .lyrdb, KLayout's report of what its DRC found, is not a layout at all - it is laid
                             over the one that is open rather than opened in its place. See openReport.
                         *@
                         <InputFile id="fileUpload" style="display: none;" OnChange="onFileInputChanged" multiple
                                    accept=".gds,.gds2,.gdsii,.oas,.oasis,.dxf,.svg,.lyrdb" />

                         @*
                             A layout to start from nothing, which every other way in needs a file for.

                             **First in the group, ahead of Open.** The two answer the same question - what
                             am I working on - and this is the answer for somebody who has nothing yet, so
                             it reads in the order the two are reached in. Off in a read-only embed for the
                             same reason opening a file is: that page has said this viewer does not take a
                             different layout, and making one is the same thing by another route.

                             A page with a fold in the corner and a plus beside it, which is what every
                             other application draws for this.
                         *@
                         <button id="newLayout"
                                 class="buttonStyle2 toolbarButton buttonIcon fileButton @(noEditing ? "toolbarButtonOff" : "")"
                                 disabled="@noEditing"
                                 @onclick="newLayout"
                                 title="@(noEditing ? "This viewer is read-only - starting a layout is turned off on this page" : "Start an empty layout: one cell, no layers, nothing drawn")">
                             <svg viewBox="0 0 16 16" width="15" height="15" aria-hidden="true" role="img" aria-label="New layout">
                                 <path fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.6"
                                       stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"
                                       d="M9.2 1.8H4.2A1.2 1.2 0 0 0 3 3v10a1.2 1.2 0 0 0 1.2 1.2H8M9.2 1.8 13 5.6v2.2M12.4 10.4v4M10.4 12.4h4" />
                             </svg>
                         </button>

                         @*
                             Turned off rather than taken away, so the bar keeps its shape and somebody can
                             see that opening a file is a thing this does - it is the page they are on that
                             has said no, not the app that lacks it.

                             A label rather than a button, so `disabled` means nothing to it: what stops the
                             click is pointer-events, and the class carries both that and the look.
                         *@
                         <label for="@(noEditing ? "" : "fileUpload")"
                                class="buttonStyle2 toolbarButton buttonIcon fileButton @(noEditing ? "toolbarButtonOff" : "")"
                                aria-disabled="@(noEditing ? "true" : "false")"
                                title="@(noEditing ? "This viewer is read-only - opening a file is turned off on this page" : "Open a GDS, OASIS, DXF or SVG file from this computer, or a KLayout report (.lyrdb) to lay over the one open")">
                             @*Into the tray, which is the way round every file dialog draws it.*@
                             <svg viewBox="0 0 16 16" width="15" height="15" aria-hidden="true" role="img" aria-label="Open a file">
                                 <path fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.6"
                                       stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"
                                       d="M8 10.6V1.8M4.8 5 8 1.8 11.2 5M2.2 10.4v2.6a1.2 1.2 0 0 0 1.2 1.2h9.2a1.2 1.2 0 0 0 1.2-1.2v-2.6" />
                             </svg>
                         </label>

                         @*
                             The download and the format it writes, as one control.

                             They were a button and a dropdown with a gap between them, which reads as two
                             things that happen to be adjacent - and one of them makes no sense without the
                             other: a format that nothing writes, and a save with no say in what it writes.
                             Joined, the way the grid's icon is joined to its pitch.
                         *@
                         <div class="joinedPair toolbarPopupHost">
                             <button id="downloadGds" class="buttonStyle2 toolbarButton buttonIcon fileButton joinedStart"
                                     @onclick="downloadFile"
                                     aria-label="Save the file"
                                     title="@downloadTitle()">
                                 <svg viewBox="0 0 16 16" width="15" height="15" aria-hidden="true">
                                     <path fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.6"
                                           stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"
                                           d="M8 1.8v8.8M4.8 7.4 8 10.6l3.2-3.2M2.2 10.4v2.6a1.2 1.2 0 0 0 1.2 1.2h9.2a1.2 1.2 0 0 0 1.2-1.2v-2.6" />
                                 </svg>
                             </button>

                             @*
                                 What the download writes, joined to the button that writes it. Starts on
                                 whatever the file arrived as, since converting is the exception and keeping
                                 the format is the rule - and the two are the same click either way.

                                 value plus @onchange rather than @bind, so the session hears about it.
                             *@
                             <select id="downloadFormat" class="formatPicker joinedEnd" value="@downloadFormat" @onchange="changeDownloadFormat"
                                     title="Which format the download button beside this writes">
                                 <option value=".gds">GDS</option>
                                 <option value=".oas">OASIS</option>
                                 <option value=".dxf">DXF</option>
                                 <option value=".svg">SVG</option>
                                 <option value=".png">PNG</option>
                                 <option value="@MasksChoice">Masks</option>
                             </select>

                             @*
                                 One bitmap a layer, for a mask writer that takes pictures rather than polygons.

                                 **Behind the button rather than in the bar**, because a mask has a pixel size and
                                 a polarity, and nothing else the picker offers asks a question. Choosing Masks and
                                 pressing save opens this; Save in it is what writes the zip. Put away by a press
                                 anywhere else - see registerBarMenu.
                             *@
                             @if (masksOpen)
                             {
                                 <div id="masksPanel" class="shapePicker masksPanel" role="group" aria-label="Masks">
                                     <span class="shapePickNote shapePickAside">
                                         A black and white PNG for each layer that is showing, all the same size and lined
                                         up with each other, in one zip.
                                     </span>

                                     <label class="shapePickField">
                                         <span>Pixel</span>

                                         <input id="maskPixel" class="gridPitch" type="number" min="0" step="any"
                                                value="@SvgWriter.FormatNumber(maskMicrons)" @onchange="changeMaskPixel"
                                                title="How big one pixel is on the mask" />

                                         <span class="gridUnit">&#181;m</span>
                                     </label>

                                     <label class="shapePickField shapePickCheck">
                                         <input id="maskLight" type="checkbox" checked="@maskShapesLight" @onchange="changeMaskShapesLight" />

                                         <span>Shapes white on black</span>
                                     </label>

                                     <span id="maskSize" class="shapePickNote shapePickAside">@masksSize()</span>

                                     <button id="saveMasks" class="buttonStyle2 smallButton shapePickGo" @onclick="saveMasks"
                                             title="Write the masks and save them as one zip">Save masks</button>
                                 </div>
                             }
                         </div>

                         @*
                             Comparing with another file, beside the buttons that open and save one.

                             **It was a third list in the side panel**, beside Layers and Rules - but those two describe
                             the file on screen, where this brings a second file in, which is what the buttons here are
                             about. So it hangs off a button of its own in this group.

                             **And it stays up**, unlike the other panels in the bar, which a press anywhere else puts
                             away. Comparing is stepping through differences while looking at them on the drawing, so a
                             press on the drawing has to leave it where it is. The button or its cross puts it away, and
                             the marks stay on the drawing either way.

                             The 2D view only, which is where the marks are drawn. Grayed in the others rather than
                             taken away, the rule this bar keeps.

                             The file chosen is kept until Clear, so an edit here can be compared again without choosing
                             it a second time, and another file opened here can be compared with it too.
                         *@
                         <div class="toolbarPopupHost">
                             <button id="compareButton" class="buttonStyle2 toolbarButton buttonIcon fileButton"
                                     @onclick="toggleCompare"
                                     disabled="@(view != ViewType.View2DSvg)"
                                     aria-label="Compare"
                                     aria-expanded="@(compareShown() ? "true" : "false")"
                                     title="@compareTitle()">
                                 @*Two outlines laid over each other, which is what comparing does to two files.*@
                                 <svg viewBox="0 0 16 16" width="15" height="15" aria-hidden="true">
                                     <rect x="1.8" y="1.8" width="8.4" height="8.4" rx="0.8"
                                           fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5" />
                                     <rect x="5.8" y="5.8" width="8.4" height="8.4" rx="0.8"
                                           fill="currentColor" fill-opacity="0.35" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5" />
                                 </svg>
                             </button>

                             @if (compareShown())
                             {
                                 <div id="comparePanel" class="shapePicker comparePanel" role="group" aria-label="Compare">
                                     <div class="comparePanelHead">
                                         <span>Compare with another file</span>

                                         <button id="compareClose" class="viewNoticeClose" @onclick="toggleCompare"
                                                 aria-label="Close the comparison" title="Close. The marks stay on the drawing">&times;</button>
                                     </div>

                                     <div class="layerSidebarControls">
                                         <InputFile id="compareImport" style="display: none;" accept=".gds,.gds2,.gdsii,.oas,.oasis,.dxf,.svg" OnChange="onCompareChosen" />
                                         <label for="compareImport" class="layerSidebarAction" title="Choose a file to compare this one with - GDS, OASIS, DXF or SVG">
                                             <img src="./resources/Images/Icons/upload.png" alt="" /> Compare with&hellip;
                                         </label>

                                         @if (otherFile is not null)
                                         {
                                             <span id="compareClear" class="layerSidebarAction layerSidebarClear" @onclick="clearCompare"
                                                   title="Put the other file away and take its marks off the drawing">Clear</span>
                                         }
                                     </div>

                                     @if (otherFile is not null)
                                     {
                                         <div class="rulesRun compareRun">
                                             <button id="compareRun" class="buttonStyle2 smallButton rulesButton" @onclick="runCompare"
                                                     title="Lay the two over each other again, as this file now stands">Compare</button>

                                             <label class="compareIgnore" title="Leave out a difference thinner than this - for two files snapped to different grids. Nothing is left out at zero">
                                                 ignore under
                                                 <input id="compareIgnore" class="gridPitch" type="number" min="0" step="any"
                                                        value="@SvgWriter.FormatNumber(compareIgnoreMicrons)" @onchange="changeCompareIgnore" />
                                                 &#181;m
                                             </label>
                                         </div>
                                     }

                                     <div class="layerList compareList">
                                         @if (otherFile is null)
                                         {
                                             <div class="rulesEmpty">
                                                 Choose a file to compare this one with: GDS, OASIS, DXF or SVG. Each
                                                 layer is laid over the same layer of the other - an XOR, the way a
                                                 foundry checks that a resubmitted file changed only what it was meant
                                                 to - and what only one of them draws is marked on the drawing, to be
                                                 stepped through. Both are flattened and merged first, so a cell
                                                 renamed or a shape cut in two is no difference at all.
                                             </div>
                                         }
                                         else if (comparison is null)
                                         {
                                             <div id="compareStale" class="rulesEmpty">@compareWaiting</div>
                                         }
                                         else
                                         {
                                             <div id="compareSummary" class="compareSummary">@compareSentence()</div>

                                             @if (!comparison.Same)
                                             {
                                                 <div class="compareSteps">
                                                     <button id="comparePrevious" class="buttonStyle2 smallButton compareStep"
                                                             @onclick="() => stepDifference(-1)" aria-label="Previous difference"
                                                             title="The difference before this one">&lsaquo;</button>

                                                     <span id="compareAt" class="compareAt">@(differenceAt + 1) of @comparison.Differences.Count</span>

                                                     <button id="compareNext" class="buttonStyle2 smallButton compareStep"
                                                             @onclick="() => stepDifference(1)" aria-label="Next difference"
                                                             title="The next difference">&rsaquo;</button>
                                                 </div>

                                                 <div id="compareCurrent" class="compareCurrent">@describeDifference(comparison.Differences[differenceAt])</div>

                                                 @*Which layers differ, and how - a row each, pressed to go to its first difference.*@
                                                 @foreach (var row in compareRows())
                                                 {
                                                     var going = row.First;

                                                     <div class="compareRow" data-layer="@row.Layer" @onclick="() => goToDifference(going)"
                                                          title="Go to the first difference on @row.Name">
                                                         <span class="compareLayer">@row.Name</span>
                                                         <span class="compareCounts">@row.Counts</span>
                                                     </div>
                                                 }
                                             }
                                         }
                                     </div>
                                 </div>
                             }
                         </div>

                     </div>

                     @*
                         The two lists of files, History first.

                         **Both open a file, so they belong together**, and the order is which one is nearer
                         the controls that also open files: History is a list of what you have had open on
                         this computer, Examples is what the app came with. Close enough together to read as
                         a pair, and one ordinary column gutter from the Open and Save buttons rather than a
                         group fence - the four of them are all about getting a file on screen, and the fence
                         belongs after them, before the controls that are about looking at it.

                         History is a clock, which is the icon everybody already knows for "what did I have
                         open before". Nothing draws "examples", so that one stays a word - at the size every
                         other button in the bar is, since the stack that made it a size smaller is gone.
                     *@
                     @*
                         **The two lists hang off these two buttons**, and go when the pointer does.

                         They were dialogs in the middle of the window with a cross in the corner, which is
                         a lot of ceremony for "what else could I open" - a question usually answered by
                         looking rather than by choosing. Pointing at the button is enough to ask it and
                         moving away is enough to stop, so there is nothing to dismiss and no cross.

                         The host is the column rather than each button, so the two lists share one place to
                         hang from and one leave to close on: the pointer crossing from History to Examples
                         never leaves the host, which is what stops the list flickering as it passes.
                     *@
                     <div class="col-1 toolbarItem toolbarGroupEnd toolbarPopupHost" style="align-self: end; display: flex; gap: 0.4em; align-items: end;"
                          @onmouseleave="closeLists">
                         @*One at a time: they are both popups over the same corner of the page.*@
                         @*
                             Off in a read-only embed. The history is a record of what has been opened on
                             this machine, and a page showing somebody else's layout has no business
                             offering that to whoever is reading it.
                         *@
                         <button id="historyButton" class="buttonStyle2 toolbarButton buttonIcon fileButton"
                                 @onclick="openHistory" @onmouseenter="openHistory"
                                 disabled="@noEditing"
                                 aria-label="History"
                                 title="@(noEditing ? "This viewer is read-only - the file history is turned off on this page" : "Files you have opened from this computer or changed, as you left them")">
                             @*
                                 Counterclockwise: the hands going back is what makes it history rather than a
                                 clock, and the arrow is the only part of the drawing carrying that.
                             *@
                             <svg viewBox="0 0 16 16" width="15" height="15" aria-hidden="true">
                                 <path fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5"
                                       stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"
                                       d="M2.2 8a5.8 5.8 0 1 1 1.7 4.1" />
                                 <path fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5"
                                       stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"
                                       d="M1.5 5.1 2.2 8.4 5.5 7.7" />
                                 <path fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5"
                                       stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"
                                       d="M8 4.8V8l2.3 1.4" />
                             </svg>
                         </button>

                         <button id="examplesButton" class="buttonStyle2 toolbarButton fileButton examplesButton"
                                 @onclick="openExamples" @onmouseenter="openExamples"
                                 title="The bundled example layouts, and links to the PDK they come from">Examples</button>

                         @*
                             Both lists live here, under the buttons that open them.

                             Rendered inside the host rather than at the foot of the page: an absolutely
                             positioned child is still a descendant, so the pointer moving from the button
                             onto the list never leaves the host and the mouseleave above does not fire.
                             Laid out at the page's end, they would have been a separate thing to leave.
                         *@
                         @if (showHistory)
                         {
                            <div class="popupDiv popupZIndex_0 popupUnder">
                                <div class="col">

                                    @*The same heading and cross as Examples; see the note there.*@
                                    <div class="popupHeading">
                                        <span class="popupHeadingName">History</span>

                                        <button id="closeHistory" class="buttonStyle2 closeButton"
                                                @onclick="hideExamples"
                                                aria-label="Close" title="Close">&times;</button>
                                    </div>

                                    <hr class="popupDivider" />

                                    <div class="row mb-3">
                                        <div class="examplePicker">
                                            <div class="examplePickerList">
                                                @*
                                                    The same filter as the example list has. This one is capped at twenty
                                                    rather than nine hundred, so it is not the difference between usable
                                                    and hopeless here - but the names in it are long and near-identical
                                                    for a run of cells out of one library, which is the case it is for.
                                                *@
                                                <input type="text"
                                                       class="examplePickerFilter historyPickerFilter"
                                                       value="@historyFilter"
                                                       placeholder="Filter @historyEntries.Count file@(historyEntries.Count == 1 ? "" : "s")"
                                                       title="Narrow the list. Matches anywhere in the name"
                                                       @oninput="onHistoryFilterInput" />

                                                <div id="historyPicker" class="examplePickerOptions" role="listbox"
                                                     @onmouseleave="listLeft">
                                                    @if (historyEntries.Count == 0)
                                                    {
                                                        @*
                                                            Says what puts something here, rather than only that nothing
                                                            is. The rule is not guessable from an empty box: an example
                                                            you only looked at is deliberately not kept.
                                                        *@
                                                        <div class="examplePickerNone">
                                                            Nothing yet. Files you open from this computer are kept here, and so is any file you change.
                                                        </div>
                                                    }
                                                    else if (filteredHistory.Count == 0)
                                                    {
                                                        <div class="examplePickerNone">Nothing matches "@historyFilter"</div>
                                                    }
                                                    else
                                                    {
                                                        @*
                                                            Not virtualized, unlike the example list. That one is nine
                                                            hundred rows; this one is capped at twenty, and Virtualize
                                                            would only put a scroll container around rows that all fit.
                                                        *@
                                                        @foreach (var entry in filteredHistory)
                                                        {
                                                            <div class="examplePickerOption historyRow @(entry.Name == CurrentlySelectedFileName + CurrentlySelectedFileType ? "isChosen" : "")"
                                                                 role="option"
                                                                 aria-selected="@((entry.Name == CurrentlySelectedFileName + CurrentlySelectedFileType).ToString().ToLowerInvariant())"
                                                                 data-file="@entry.Name"
                                                                 @onmouseenter="() => historyPreviewOnHover(entry.Name)">

                                                                @*
                                                                    The name is what opens the row, rather than the row
                                                                    itself: a click anywhere on the row would also be a
                                                                    click on the delete button sitting in it, and
                                                                    deleting a file would then open it on the way out.
                                                                *@
                                                                <span class="historyRowName" title="@rowTitle(entry)" @onclick="() => openFromHistory(entry.Name)">@entry.Name</span>

                                                                @if (entry.Edited)
                                                                {
                                                                    <span class="historyRowEdited" title="The records in this file were changed here">edited</span>
                                                                }

                                                                <button class="historyRowForget" @onclick="() => forgetFromHistory(entry.Name)"
                                                                        title="Forget @entry.Name">&times;</button>
                                                            </div>
                                                        }
                                                    }
                                                </div>
                                            </div>

                                            @*
                                                The same frame as the example picker's, drawn from the same preview code, and as tall as the
                                                list beside it - its box is in the line under the popup, beside Clear History, as the
                                                examples popup's is at the end of its own (2026-10-21).
                                            *@
                                            <div class="examplePreviewColumn">
                                                <div class="examplePreviewFrame @(previewDrawing ? "isDrawing" : "")">
                                                    @if (previewPicture.Length > 0)
                                                    {
                                                        <img class="examplePreview" src="@previewPicture" alt="Preview of @previewShownFor" />
                                                    }
                                                    else if (previewWaiting && historyPreviewFor.Length > 0)
                                                    {
                                                        <button id="historyPreviewGenerate" class="previewGenerate" @onclick="generatePreview"
                                                                title="Draw @historyPreviewFor">
                                                            <span class="previewGenerateSays">Generate preview</span>
                                                            <span class="previewGenerateName">@historyPreviewFor</span>
                                                        </button>
                                                    }
                                                    else if (historyPreviewFor.Length == 0)
                                                    {
                                                        @*
                                                            Nothing is being pointed at, which is not the same as a file that has no
                                                            picture - so it does not say "No preview", which would be a sentence about a
                                                            file nobody named. The wording is the cell tree's, for the same empty state.
                                                        *@
                                                        <div class="examplePreviewEmpty">Point at a file</div>
                                                    }
                                                    else if (!previewDrawing)
                                                    {
                                                        <div class="examplePreviewEmpty">No preview</div>
                                                    }

                                                    @*While a picture is drawn, after a moment - see the Examples frame below.*@
                                                    @if (previewDrawing)
                                                    {
                                                        <div class="previewDrawing" role="status">
                                                            <span class="viewLoadingSpinner" aria-hidden="true"></span>
                                                            <span>Drawing @historyPreviewFor</span>
                                                        </div>
                                                    }
                                                </div>
                                            </div>
                                        </div>
                                    </div>

                                    @*
                                        Under the list rather than up beside the close button.

                                        Emptying the whole list is the one thing in this popup that cannot be undone,
                                        and up in the heading it sat next to the control people reach for to dismiss a
                                        dialog without reading it. Down here it is where you arrive after looking at
                                        what you would be throwing away.

                                        Only offered when there is something to clear, so it is not a permanent
                                        invitation to press something that would do nothing.

                                        **Auto-generate preview on the same line, under the picture** (2026-10-21), as each
                                        is under what it is about. It was under the picture inside its column, a line of its
                                        own out of the picture's height, so the picture stopped short of the list beside it.
                                        It goes with the line: with nothing listed there is nothing to draw either.
                                    *@
                                    @if (historyEntries.Count > 0)
                                    {
                                        <div class="historyFoot">
                                            <div class="historyFootList">
                                                <button id="clearHistoryButton" class="buttonStyle2 smallButton" @onclick="clearHistory"
                                                        title="Forget every file listed here. The files on your computer are untouched">Clear History</button>
                                            </div>

                                            <div class="historyFootPreview">
                                                <label class="previewAuto" title="@autoPreviewSays()">
                                                    <input id="historyAutoPreview" type="checkbox" checked="@autoPreview" @onchange="autoPreviewToggled" />
                                                    Auto-generate preview
                                                </label>
                                            </div>
                                        </div>
                                    }

                                </div>
                            </div>
                         }

                         @if (showExamples)
                         {
                            <div class="popupDiv popupZIndex_0 popupUnder">
                                <div class="col">

                                    @*
                                        The heading, and a cross.

                                        **The cross came back.** This popup closes when the pointer leaves
                                        it, which is quick once you know it and is nothing at all if you do
                                        not: there is no control to look for, so the way out has to be
                                        discovered by accident. Both now - the move still works, and the
                                        cross is there for anybody who reaches for one.

                                        A flex row rather than two bootstrap columns. Two `col`s split the
                                        width in half and the heading took the left one, which left a band
                                        of empty column across the top of the popup wide enough to read as
                                        a missing control.
                                    *@
                                    <div class="popupHeading">
                                        <span class="popupHeadingName">Examples</span>

                                        <button id="closeExamples" class="buttonStyle2 closeButton"
                                                @onclick="hideExamples"
                                                aria-label="Close" title="Close">&times;</button>
                                    </div>

                                    @*Bracketing the heading off from the body, the same way About does.*@
                                    <hr class="popupDivider" />

                                    <div class="row mb-3">
                                        <div class="examplePicker">
                                            @*
                                                Rows rather than a select, so pointing at one can show it.

                                                An <option> is drawn by the operating system and reports nothing back
                                                to the page - no hover, no focus - so a native picker could only ever
                                                preview what had already been chosen. These are divs, so they can.

                                                Virtualized, because there are nine hundred of them and rendering that
                                                many on every keystroke in the filter box is not free. Filtered too,
                                                which is what makes a list this long usable at all: the names differ by
                                                a few characters at the end, so scrolling for one is hopeless.
                                            *@
                                            <div class="examplePickerList">
                                                <input type="text"
                                                       class="examplePickerFilter"
                                                       value="@exampleFilter"
                                                       placeholder="Filter @((exampleFiles.Count + pdkExamples.Sum(pdk => pdk.Cells.Count) + designs.Count + 1).ToString()) examples"
                                                       title="Narrow the list. Matches anywhere in the name"
                                                       @oninput="onExampleFilterInput" />

                                                <div id="examplePicker" class="examplePickerOptions" role="listbox"
                                                     @onmouseleave="listLeft">
                                                    @if (filteredExamples.Count == 0)
                                                    {
                                                        <div class="examplePickerNone">Nothing matches "@exampleFilter"</div>
                                                    }
                                                    else
                                                    {
                                                        <Virtualize Items="filteredExamples" Context="row" ItemSize="ExampleRowHeight" OverscanCount="6">
                                                            @if (row.IsHeading)
                                                            {
                                                                <div class="examplePickerHeading">@row.Name</div>
                                                            }
                                                            else
                                                            {
                                                                <div class="examplePickerOption @(row.Name == selectedExample ? "isChosen" : "")"
                                                                     role="option"
                                                                     aria-selected="@((row.Name == selectedExample).ToString().ToLowerInvariant())"
                                                                     data-file="@row.Name"
                                                                     title="@exampleTooltip(row.Name)"
                                                                     @onmouseenter="() => previewOnHover(row.Name)"
                                                                     @onclick="() => openExample(row.Name)">@if (downloadedDesign(row.Name) is BundledDesign fetched)
                                                                    {
                                                                        <span class="exampleDownloadSize" aria-hidden="true">@GDSViewer.Ai.AiAttachment.Size(fetched.Size)</span>
                                                                    }@row.Name</div>
                                                            }
                                                        </Virtualize>
                                                    }
                                                </div>
                                            </div>

                                            @*
                                                What was chosen, drawn, beside the list that chose it. The popup stays
                                                open while cells are looked through, so this is the difference between
                                                reading a list of names and seeing what they are.

                                                On selection rather than on hover: an <option> in a native select is
                                                drawn by the operating system and reports nothing back to the page -
                                                no hover, no focus, nothing until it is picked.
                                            *@
                                            @*
                                                A frame that stretches to the height of the list beside it, with the
                                                drawing filling it. The frame is what carries the border and the size,
                                                so the picture has only to fill its parent, fitted inside it.
                                            *@
                                            @*
                                                In a column of its own, the way the history popup's frame is with its box under
                                                it; this one's box is at the end of the line under the popup - see below.
                                            *@
                                            @*
                                                **A picture, not markup on the page**, since 2026-10-17: a PNG drawn at the frame's
                                                own size, or a small file's SVG as a document of its own - see PreviewPictures. And
                                                **while one is drawn** the picture before it fades and a
                                                note says so, both after a moment, so a cell drawn in a few milliseconds swaps one
                                                picture for the next without either.
                                            *@
                                            <div class="examplePreviewColumn">
                                                <div class="examplePreviewFrame @(previewDrawing ? "isDrawing" : "")">
                                                    @if (previewPicture.Length > 0)
                                                    {
                                                        <img class="examplePreview" src="@previewPicture" alt="Preview of @previewShownFor" />
                                                    }
                                                    else if (previewWaiting && previewFor.Length > 0)
                                                    {
                                                        <button id="examplePreviewGenerate" class="previewGenerate" @onclick="generatePreview"
                                                                title="@previewGenerateTitle()">
                                                            <span class="previewGenerateSays">@previewGenerateSays()</span>
                                                            <span class="previewGenerateName">@previewFor</span>
                                                        </button>
                                                    }
                                                    else if (previewFor.Length == 0)
                                                    {
                                                        @*Nothing pointed at. See the history popup's frame for why the two read differently.*@
                                                        <div class="examplePreviewEmpty">Point at a file</div>
                                                    }
                                                    else if (!previewDrawing)
                                                    {
                                                        <div class="examplePreviewEmpty">No preview</div>
                                                    }

                                                    @if (previewDrawing)
                                                    {
                                                        <div class="previewDrawing" role="status">
                                                            <span class="viewLoadingSpinner" aria-hidden="true"></span>
                                                            <span>Drawing @previewFor</span>
                                                        </div>
                                                    }
                                                </div>
                                            </div>
                                        </div>
                                    </div>

                                    @*
                                        Under the list, about what is open: who made a design and where it came from,
                                        or the PDK's documentation for a sky130, a GF180 or an IHP cell.

                                        A design gets its credit instead of the PDK links. Those are about the standard
                                        cells, and a design is somebody else's work under its own license, which is the
                                        thing worth linking to. Both links are to the exact project and file, so anybody
                                        can go and get the design from where it came from.

                                        **And Auto-generate preview at the end of that line** - see autoPreview. It was
                                        under the picture, a line of its own out of the picture's height; here it shares
                                        the line with the credit, level with its first line, and the line is there for
                                        every file, the credit or none.
                                    *@
                                    <hr class="popupDivider" />

                                    <div class="examplesFoot">
                                        <div class="examplesFootAbout">
                                            @if (openDesign() is BundledDesign design)
                                            {
                                                <div class="designCredit">
                                                    <p class="mb-1"><b>@design.Title</b>, by @design.Author</p>
                                                    <p class="mb-1">@design.About</p>
                                                    <p class="mb-0">
                                                        <a href="@design.Project" target="_blank" rel="noopener"
                                                           title="Where the design lives - its own repository. Opens in a new tab">Project</a>
                                                        <span> · </span>
                                                        <a href="@design.Gds" target="_blank" rel="noopener"
                                                           title="The file bundled here, at the commit it was taken from. Opens in a new tab">GDS file</a>
                                                        <span> · </span>
                                                        <a href="@licenseAddress(design)" target="_blank"
                                                           title="The license it is published under, which travels with it. Opens in a new tab">@design.License</a>
                                                    </p>
                                                </div>
                                            }
                                            else if (CurrentlySelectedFileName.Contains("sky130"))
                                            {
                                                <p class="mb-0">
                                                    <a href="https://skywater-pdk.readthedocs.io/en/main/contents/libraries/foundry-provided.html" target="_blank"
                                                       title="SkyWater's own documentation for the sky130 cell libraries. Opens in a new tab">PDK Docs</a>
                                                    <span>, </span>
                                                    <a href="https://skywater-pdk.readthedocs.io/en/main/contents/libraries/sky130_fd_sc_hd/cells/nand2/README.html#sky130-fd-sc-hd-nand2" target="_blank"
                                                       title="What one sky130 cell's documentation looks like, using the NAND2 as the example. Opens in a new tab">Example PDK NAND2 Cell Docs</a>
                                                </p>
                                            }
                                            else if (CurrentlySelectedFileName.Contains("gf180mcu_fd_sc"))
                                            {
                                                <p class="mb-0">
                                                    <a href="https://gf180mcu-pdk.readthedocs.io/en/latest/digital/standard_cells/gf180mcu_fd_sc_mcu7t5v0/index.html" target="_blank"
                                                       title="GlobalFoundries' own documentation for the gf180mcu 7-track cells. Opens in a new tab">PDK Docs</a>
                                                    <span>, </span>
                                                    <a href="https://gf180mcu-pdk.readthedocs.io/en/latest/physical_verification/design_manual/Design_Manual.html" target="_blank"
                                                       title="The GF180MCU design manual: its layers and its rules. Opens in a new tab">Design Manual</a>
                                                </p>
                                            }
                                            else if (CurrentlySelectedFileName.StartsWith("sg13g2_"))
                                            {
                                                <p class="mb-0">
                                                    <a href="https://github.com/IHP-GmbH/IHP-Open-PDK/tree/main/ihp-sg13g2/libs.ref/sg13g2_stdcell/doc" target="_blank"
                                                       title="IHP's own documentation for the SG13G2 standard cells: a datasheet for each corner. Opens in a new tab">PDK Docs</a>
                                                    <span>, </span>
                                                    <a href="https://github.com/IHP-GmbH/IHP-Open-PDK/blob/main/ihp-sg13g2/libs.doc/doc/SG13G2_os_layout_rules.pdf" target="_blank"
                                                       title="The SG13G2 layout rules: its layers and its rules. Opens in a new tab">Layout Rules</a>
                                                </p>
                                            }
                                        </div>

                                        <label class="previewAuto examplesFootAuto" title="@autoPreviewSays()">
                                            <input id="examplesAutoPreview" type="checkbox" checked="@autoPreview" @onchange="autoPreviewToggled" />
                                            Auto-generate preview
                                        </label>
                                    </div>
                                </div>
                            </div>
                         }
                     </div>

                     @*
                         Which view is on, as three boxes with one of them lit.

                         **A switch rather than a menu.** There are exactly three, they never change, and the
                         whole set fits in the room one word takes - so hiding two of them behind a press was
                         charging a click and a wait for something that was already affordable. This is the
                         same shape as the tool group two columns along, which is the other place in the bar
                         where one of a fixed few is chosen, and it says the same thing: these are
                         alternatives, and that one is current.

                         It was a menu before that, and a native select before that. The select was the one
                         control up here drawn by the operating system rather than by the page; the menu
                         fixed that and kept the click nobody needed.

                         `Txt` rather than `Text`, because the three read as a set when they are the same
                         length and the one that is a word among two abbreviations is the odd one.
                     *@
                     <div class="col-1 toolbarItem toolbarView toolbarGroupEnd" style="align-self: end;">
                         @*
                             role=group and aria-pressed, not a radio group: these are buttons that do
                             something when pressed rather than a field with a value, which is what a screen
                             reader is told either way and is the truer of the two here - pressing one
                             switches the screen rather than recording an answer.
                         *@
                         <div id="viewPick" class="toolGroup" role="group" aria-label="View">
                             @foreach ((ViewType which, string says, string shorthand) in Views)
                             {
                                 if (which == ViewType.View3D)
                                 {
                                     @*
                                         **The 3D view's settings hang from the cube** while that view is on: pointed at
                                         or pressed, it opens them under it - how far the view draws, its textures, Admire
                                         and the backdrop. They had a button of their own along the bar, and the backdrop
                                         another, both of them the 3D view's alone, where the cube is the one control in
                                         the bar that already means that view.

                                         In a box of its own, which the menu hangs from and which the pointer leaving puts
                                         it away, the way every panel in the bar goes - see .viewPickMenu in app.css.
                                     *@
                                     <span class="viewPickMenu" @onmouseleave="viewSettingsLeft">
                                         @viewPickBox(which, says, shorthand)

                                         @if (view == ViewType.View3D && viewer is Viewer3D three && three.ViewSettingsOpen)
                                         {
                                             @three.SettingsMenu
                                         }
                                     </span>
                                 }
                                 else
                                 {
                                     @viewPickBox(which, says, shorthand)
                                 }
                             }
                         </div>
                     </div>

                    @*
                        The two sidebars, shown or hidden.

                        Beside the view buttons and not joined to them: those three are one control with a
                        chosen segment - exactly one view is on - and these two are switches that are on or
                        off independently. Butting all five together would say they were the same kind of
                        thing, which is the one claim a joined group makes.

                        In the shell rather than in the 2D view's own stretch of the bar, though the cell
                        tree is drawn by that view. Both lists stand beside the 3D view as well, and a pair
                        of buttons that existed in one view only would be two panels you could hide from one
                        screen and not get back from the other.

                        In the text view too, since the text has a cell list of its own beside it now - the
                        same switch, so the cells are one thing to show or hide whichever view they are in.
                    *@
                    @if (!viewerOnly)
                    {
                        <div class="col-1 toolbarItem toolbarGroupEnd" style="align-self: end; display: flex; gap: 0.4em;">
                            <div class="toolGroup seamedGroup" role="group" aria-label="Sidebars">
                                @*
                                    On in all three views since 2026-10-17: the 2D view and the 3D one each draw the tree
                                    beside them, and the text view its list. It was grayed out in 3D, which had none.
                                *@
                                <button id="cellTreeButton" class="buttonStyle2 toolbarButton toolButton buttonIcon @(cellTreeOpen ? "toolButtonOn" : "")"
                                        @onclick="toggleCellTree"
                                        aria-pressed="@(cellTreeOpen ? "true" : "false")"
                                        aria-label="Cells"
                                        title="@cellsSwitchSays()">
                                    @*
                                        A trunk down the left, three branches of one length, a node on each.

                                        **The nodes are what makes it a tree rather than a bracket.** Four
                                        attempts before this were lines alone - a trunk with stubs coming off
                                        it - and a line meeting a line at a right angle is a bracket, an E, or
                                        a panel with a divider in it, which is what each of them was taken
                                        for. A branch that ends in a *thing* is a branch that leads somewhere,
                                        and three of them hanging off one trunk is the shape every file
                                        explorer draws.

                                        **One length for all three**, which is the correction that prompted
                                        this: they were a long one and two short, so the eye read a heading
                                        with items under it rather than three siblings.

                                        The trunk starts below the top of the box and turns into the bottom
                                        branch at a real corner, so the whole of the left side is one stroke.
                                        A tree's last child has nothing below it, and the turn is what says
                                        so - drawn as a separate stub it read as a mark that had come adrift.
                                    *@
                                    @*
                                        Three nodes on centers 3.2, 8 and 12.8; three branches at those same
                                        three heights, each from the trunk at x 3 to the nodes at x 7.6. The
                                        numbers are written out because they have to agree - a branch half a
                                        unit off its node is a line that stops just short of the box, which
                                        at this size is the difference between joined and nearly joined.
                                    *@
                                    <svg viewBox="0 0 16 16" width="15" height="15" aria-hidden="true">
                                        <path fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5"
                                              stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"
                                              d="M3 1.2V11.4a1.4 1.4 0 0 0 1.4 1.4H7.6M3 3.2H7.6M3 8H7.6" />

                                        @*
                                            Rounded, and filled rather than outlined at this size: a 7.4 by
                                            3.6 box with a 1.5 stroke leaves about half a pixel of hole in the
                                            middle of it, which paints as a smudge rather than as an outline.
                                        *@
                                        <rect x="7.6" y="1.4" width="7.4" height="3.6" rx="1.3" fill="currentColor" />
                                        <rect x="7.6" y="6.2" width="7.4" height="3.6" rx="1.3" fill="currentColor" />
                                        <rect x="7.6" y="11" width="7.4" height="3.6" rx="1.3" fill="currentColor" />
                                    </svg>
                                </button>

                                @*
                                    On in every view. It was off in the text view, whose records are not layers, until the
                                    rules were wanted beside the text: a check after an edit to the records says there what
                                    the edit broke - and the list names the layer numbers the text is written in.
                                *@
                                <button id="layersToggle" class="buttonStyle2 toolbarButton toolButton buttonIcon @(layersOpen ? "toolButtonOn" : "")"
                                        @onclick="toggleLayers"
                                        aria-pressed="@(layersOpen ? "true" : "false")"
                                        aria-label="Layers"
                                        title="@layersSwitchSays()">
                                    @*A stack of sheets, which is what a layer list is a list of.*@
                                    <svg viewBox="0 0 16 16" width="15" height="15" aria-hidden="true">
                                        <path fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"
                                              stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"
                                              d="M8 1.6 1.8 4.8 8 8l6.2-3.2zM1.8 8.4 8 11.6l6.2-3.2M1.8 11.8 8 15l6.2-3.2" />
                                    </svg>
                                </button>
                            </div>

                            @*
                                **The AI assistant**, after the two panels and in the same place in all three views: its window is the
                                page's rather than a view's, and works on the file whichever view is showing it.

                                It was a spark on the 2D drawing beside the camera, because this bar was full; the frame grew
                                by the button to make room - see #mainAppContainer in Viewer.razor.css. A switch like the two
                                beside it, lit while its window is up, in a group of its own since it is not one of the panels.
                                Off rather than gone on a read-only page, like every control that would change the file.
                            *@
                            <div class="toolGroup">
                                <button id="askAiButton" class="buttonStyle2 toolbarButton toolButton buttonIcon @(askAiOpen ? "toolButtonOn" : "")"
                                        @onclick="toggleAskAi"
                                        disabled="@noEditing"
                                        aria-pressed="@(askAiOpen ? "true" : "false")"
                                        aria-label="AI assistant"
                                        title="@askAiSays()">
                                    @*A spark of four points with a smaller one beside it, which is what assistants are drawn as.*@
                                    <svg viewBox="0 0 16 16" width="15" height="15" aria-hidden="true">
                                        <path fill="currentColor" d="M6.4 1.6c.3 2.6 1.6 4 4.2 4.3-2.6.3-3.9 1.7-4.2 4.3-.3-2.6-1.6-4-4.2-4.3 2.6-.3 3.9-1.7 4.2-4.3z" />
                                        <path fill="currentColor" d="M11.6 8.6c.2 1.6 1 2.4 2.6 2.6-1.6.2-2.4 1-2.6 2.6-.2-1.6-1-2.4-2.6-2.6 1.6-.2 2.4-1 2.6-2.6z" />
                                    </svg>
                                </button>
                            </div>
                        </div>
                    }


                     @viewer?.Toolbar

                     @*
                         The view, with the page's margins taken away.

                         At the end of the bar, before only the settings gear, because it is about the window
                         rather than about the file - everything before this column changes what is drawn or what
                         is being done to it, and this changes how much room there is to do it in.

                         Not the browser's own fullscreen. That hides the tab strip and the address bar,
                         which is the browser's business and needs a user gesture it can refuse; this is the
                         four rems of padding the page puts around itself, which is what is actually in the
                         way. The top bar stays either way, since it is the thing being kept.

                         On the line with everything else, at the far end of it. It was up in the corner,
                         which read as deliberate while it was a white button among white ones and read as
                         stranded once the rest of the bar became one row of squares of one size. Apart from
                         them by where it sits, not by how high it is.
                     *@
                     <div class="col-1 toolbarItem toolbarEnd">
                         @*
                             Share first, then the two about the window, and the app's own settings last of all.

                             **Down here rather than in the title bar**, which has the width but is the one thing on
                             the page somebody can put away - and would take these with it. This bar is pinned to
                             one line at 1280 pixels and had about twenty to spare, so the room for them came from
                             the page: see #mainAppContainer in Viewer.razor.css, which keeps the editor wide
                             enough for the whole bar on one line.

                             None of the four at this end is about the file - Share and the gear are about the app and
                             the two between them about the window - which is why they share it.

                             The PDK manager's chip stood beside Share from 2026-10-18 to the next day, when it went: the
                             PDK button over the layer panel and the gear's own line for it are the ways in now.
                         *@
                         <button id="shareButton" class="iconButton toolbarEndButton buttonIcon" @onclick="openShare"
                                 aria-label="Share" aria-haspopup="dialog"
                                 title="Share a link to the viewer, or the code that embeds it in a page">
                             @*Three points joined, which is the mark people already read as "share".*@
                             <svg viewBox="0 0 16 16" aria-hidden="true">
                                 <circle cx="12" cy="3.4" r="2" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4" />
                                 <circle cx="4" cy="8" r="2" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4" />
                                 <circle cx="12" cy="12.6" r="2" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4" />
                                 <path d="M5.8 7 10.2 4.4M5.8 9 10.2 11.6" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4" />
                             </svg>
                         </button>

                         @*
                             The room either side of the pair: twenty pixels where the line has it, twelve where
                             it has not. A box of its own, since a gap cannot grow; see .toolbarEndSpace in app.css.
                         *@
                         <span class="toolbarEndSpace" aria-hidden="true"></span>

                         @*
                             The two about the window, as a pair: the title bar, and the page's margins.

                             A box of their own, so the end of the bar is spaced as three things rather than
                             four: Share before the pair and the gear after it stand further off than the two
                             in it do from each other. See .toolbarWindowPair in app.css.
                         *@
                         <div class="toolbarWindowPair">
                             @*
                                 The title bar, shown or hidden.

                                 Beside full screen because it is the same kind of decision - how much of the
                                 window the drawing gets - and the smaller of the two: the banner is one band at
                                 the top, where full screen is every margin the page has.

                                 The layout owns the banner and this page owns the bar, so the press goes up
                                 through a cascade; see MainLayout.ToggleBanner. Left out entirely when the
                                 address has already said no banner, because a switch that cannot turn anything
                                 on is a switch that does nothing.

                                 That last part was a comment and not a condition: this asked BannerShowing,
                                 which is also false while somebody has merely put the banner away - so it could
                                 not be the test, and an embed on banner=false got the button anyway. See
                                 MainLayout.BannerCanShow, which is the question this needed.
                             *@
                             @if (Shell is not null && Shell.BannerCanShow && !viewerOnly)
                             {
                                 <button id="bannerToggle" class="iconButton toolbarEndButton buttonIcon"
                                         @onclick="() => Shell.ToggleBanner()"
                                         aria-pressed="@(Shell.BannerShowing ? "true" : "false")"
                                         aria-label="Title bar"
                                         title="@(Shell.BannerShowing ? "Hide the title bar" : "Show the title bar")">
                                     @*
                                         A band across the top of a page, which is what the banner is - filled
                                         when it is showing and an outline when it is not, so the button says
                                         which state it is in rather than which one it would take you to.
                                     *@
                                     <svg viewBox="0 0 16 16" aria-hidden="true">
                                         <rect x="1.6" y="2.4" width="12.8" height="11.2" rx="1.4"
                                               fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4" />
                                         <path d="M1.6 6.2h12.8" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4" />
                                         @if (Shell.BannerShowing)
                                         {
                                             <rect x="1.6" y="2.4" width="12.8" height="3.8" rx="1.4" fill="currentColor" />
                                         }
                                     </svg>
                                 </button>
                             }

                             <button id="fullScreen" class="iconButton toolbarEndButton buttonIcon" @onclick="toggleFullScreen"
                                     aria-label="@(fullScreen ? "Leave full screen" : "Full screen")"
                                     title="@(fullScreen ? "Leave full screen" : "Full screen the editor")">
                                 <svg viewBox="0 0 16 16" aria-hidden="true">
                                     @if (fullScreen)
                                     {
                                         <path fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.6" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"
                                               d="M6.5 1.5v5h-5M9.5 14.5v-5h5" />
                                     }
                                     else
                                     {
                                         <path fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.6" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"
                                               d="M1.5 6V1.5H6M10 1.5h4.5V6M14.5 10v4.5H10M6 14.5H1.5V10" />
                                     }
                                 </svg>
                             </button>
                         </div>

                         <span class="toolbarEndSpace" aria-hidden="true"></span>

                         @*
                             The app's own settings, behind a gear: light or dark, and the frame rate.

                             **A drop-down rather than a dialog**, the way the grid's switches are: what is in it
                             is set once and left, and a dialog in the middle of the window is a lot of ceremony
                             for a switch. Opened by a press and by pointing at it; put away by a press anywhere
                             else - see registerBarMenu - or by the pointer leaving it.
                         *@
                         <div class="toolbarPopupHost" @onmouseleave="closeAppSettings">
                             <button id="settingsMenu" class="iconButton toolbarEndButton buttonIcon"
                                     @onclick="openAppSettings"
                                     @onmouseenter="openAppSettings"
                                     aria-label="Settings"
                                     aria-expanded="@(appSettingsOpen ? "true" : "false")"
                                     title="Settings">
                                 @*The layer rows' gear, with its hole cut through rather than painted over.*@
                                 <svg viewBox="0 0 16 16" aria-hidden="true">
                                     <path fill="currentColor" fill-rule="evenodd" d="M6.8 1h2.4l.3 1.9.9.5 1.7-.9 1.7 1.7-.9 1.7.5.9 1.9.3v2.4l-1.9.3-.5.9.9 1.7-1.7 1.7-1.7-.9-.9.5-.3 1.9H6.8l-.3-1.9-.9-.5-1.7.9-1.7-1.7.9-1.7-.5-.9L.7 9.2V6.8l1.9-.3.5-.9-.9-1.7 1.7-1.7 1.7.9.9-.5L6.8 1zM10.4 8a2.4 2.4 0 1 1-4.8 0 2.4 2.4 0 1 1 4.8 0z" />
                                 </svg>
                             </button>

                             @if (appSettingsOpen)
                             {
                                 <div id="settingsPicker" class="shapePicker settingsPicker" role="group" aria-label="Settings">
                                     @*
                                         A switch rather than a pair of choices, because there are two looks and
                                         one of them is the exception: dark is on or it is off. Bootstrap's own, so
                                         it is the switch a visitor has met before.
                                     *@
                                     <div class="form-check form-switch settingsSwitch"
                                          title="A dark page, panels and drawing ground. Remembered on this computer">
                                         <input id="darkMode" class="form-check-input" type="checkbox" role="switch"
                                                checked="@darkMode" @onchange="darkModeToggled" />
                                         <label class="form-check-label" for="darkMode">Dark mode</label>
                                     </div>

                                     @*
                                         For finding out whether a view keeps up, rather than guessing from the feel
                                         of it. Named for what it is to somebody who has not met it - information
                                         for chasing a problem - with what it shows in the tooltip.
                                     *@
                                     <div class="form-check form-switch settingsSwitch"
                                          title="Frames a second in the 2D and 3D views, with a graph of the last ten seconds and the longest frame, in the drawing's top left corner. Remembered on this computer">
                                         <input id="debugInfo" class="form-check-input" type="checkbox" role="switch"
                                                checked="@debugInfo" @onchange="debugInfoToggled" />
                                         <label class="form-check-label" for="debugInfo">Debug info</label>
                                     </div>

                                     @*
                                         The assistant's models, in a window of their own: a list and a form, which is
                                         more than a switch can hold. The conversation opens from the bar - the spark
                                         after the Cells and Layers switches - and this is where it gets a model to talk to.
                                     *@
                                     <div class="settingsRule"></div>

                                     @*
                                         Each with a picture in front, as Play game has - asked for 2026-10-19, with the "…" each
                                         carried after its words taken off. The spark is the bar's own for the assistant.
                                     *@
                                     <button id="aiSettingsItem" class="shapePick settingsItem settingsItemIcon" @onclick="openAiSettings"
                                             title="The AI models the assistant can use, with your own keys, kept in this browser - and the MCP server, which lets Claude work on this tab">
                                         <svg class="shapePickIcon" viewBox="0 0 16 16" width="14" height="14" aria-hidden="true">
                                             <path fill="currentColor" d="M6.4 1.6c.3 2.6 1.6 4 4.2 4.3-2.6.3-3.9 1.7-4.2 4.3-.3-2.6-1.6-4-4.2-4.3 2.6-.3 3.9-1.7 4.2-4.3z" />
                                             <path fill="currentColor" d="M11.6 8.6c.2 1.6 1 2.4 2.6 2.6-1.6.2-2.4 1-2.6 2.6-.2-1.6-1-2.4-2.6-2.6 1.6-.2 2.4-1 2.6-2.6z" />
                                         </svg>
                                         AI settings
                                     </button>

                                     @*The PDKs - sky130 and any of your own - each with its layers and rules. See PdkManager. The chip the PDK button over the layer panel has.*@
                                     <button id="pdkManagerItem" class="shapePick settingsItem settingsItemIcon" @onclick="openPdkManager"
                                             title="Your PDKs, each with its layers and rules, and which one is in use. Kept in this browser">
                                         <svg class="shapePickIcon" viewBox="0 0 16 16" width="14" height="14" aria-hidden="true">
                                             <rect x="4.2" y="4.2" width="7.6" height="7.6" rx="1.2" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4" />
                                             <path d="M6.4 1.6v2.6M9.6 1.6v2.6M6.4 11.8v2.6M9.6 11.8v2.6M1.6 6.4h2.6M1.6 9.6h2.6M11.8 6.4h2.6M11.8 9.6h2.6"
                                                   fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4" stroke-linecap="round" />
                                         </svg>
                                         PDK manager
                                     </button>

                                     @*The guided tour, which never starts on its own - see GuidedTour. A compass, for finding your way round.*@
                                     <button id="tourItem" class="shapePick settingsItem settingsItemIcon" @onclick="startTour"
                                             title="A short walk round the app: where things are and what they are for. Esc ends it">
                                         <svg class="shapePickIcon" viewBox="0 0 16 16" width="14" height="14" aria-hidden="true">
                                             <circle cx="8" cy="8" r="6.3" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.3" />
                                             <path d="M10.7 5.3 9.1 9.1 5.3 10.7 6.9 6.9z" fill="currentColor" />
                                         </svg>
                                         Take the tour
                                     </button>

                                     @*
                                         The chip game, a little car to drive over the layers in 3D - see ChipGame in
                                         js/ThreeInterop.js. From another view it goes to 3D first; see playGame.
                                         Not without a file, which leaves nothing to drive on.
                                     *@
                                     @if (gds is not null)
                                     {
                                         <button id="playGameItem" class="shapePick settingsItem settingsItemIcon" @onclick="playGame"
                                                 title="Drive a little car over the layers in 3D and jump from one to the next. Esc stops it">
                                             @*
                                                 A game pad: the body and its two grips, a cross on the left and two
                                                 buttons on the right. The outline is what says it at fourteen pixels;
                                                 the cross and the buttons are what make it a pad rather than a mask.
                                             *@
                                             <svg class="shapePickIcon" viewBox="0 0 16 16" width="14" height="14" aria-hidden="true">
                                                 <path fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.3" stroke-linejoin="round"
                                                       d="M5 3.8h6c1.6 0 2.8 1.1 3.1 2.8l.7 4.3c.2 1.3-.6 2.3-1.7 2.3-.7 0-1.3-.4-1.7-1.1l-.9-1.5H5.5l-.9 1.5c-.4.7-1 1.1-1.7 1.1-1.1 0-1.9-1-1.7-2.3l.7-4.3C2.2 4.9 3.4 3.8 5 3.8z" />
                                                 <path fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.3" stroke-linecap="round"
                                                       d="M5.2 6.2v2.4M4 7.4h2.4" />
                                                 <circle cx="10.1" cy="8.1" r="0.85" fill="currentColor" />
                                                 <circle cx="11.7" cy="6.7" r="0.85" fill="currentColor" />
                                             </svg>
                                             @(gamePlaying ? "Stop the game" : "Play game")
                                         </button>

                                         @*The game's panel of keys and options, back after its × put it away - only while a game runs.*@
                                         @if (gamePlaying)
                                         {
                                             <button id="gameControlsItem" class="shapePick settingsItem settingsSubItem" @onclick="showGameControls"
                                                     title="The game's keys and its options, in the panel its × put away">
                                                 Game controls
                                             </button>
                                         }
                                     }

                                     @*
                                         About, last and set apart: the popup the title bar's link opens. Here as well,
                                         because the title bar is not always there - an embed that asked for none,
                                         viewer-only, or put away by its button - and then this is the way to it. Left
                                         out on a page outside the shell's layout, which has no About to open.
                                     *@
                                     @if (Shell is not null)
                                     {
                                         <div class="settingsRule"></div>

                                         <button id="aboutItem" class="shapePick settingsItem" @onclick="openAbout"
                                                 title="What this app is, who made it, and the terms it is offered under">
                                             About
                                         </button>
                                     }
                                 </div>
                             }
                         </div>
                     </div>

                 </div>
             </div>
            }

            @*
                What went wrong with a layermap the address asked for.

                Only ever shown when one was named and could not be used, which for an ordinary visit is
                never. Dismissable, like the download notice: it is about something that has finished, and
                the visitor cannot fix it - see fetchLayerMap for why it is here rather than in an alert.
            *@
            @if (layerMapNotice.Length > 0)
            {
                <div id="layerMapNotice" class="row inlineNotice">
                    <div class="col">@layerMapNotice</div>
                    <div class="col-auto">
                        <button class="buttonStyle2 closeButton" @onclick="dismissLayerMapNotice" title="Dismiss">&times;</button>
                    </div>
                </div>
            }

            @*
                What the file turned out not to be.

                **Three things the flattener has always worked out and nobody was ever told.** Only the CLI
                read them; the app opened a truncated layout, a layout with a reference loop in it, or one
                missing the cells it points at, and drew what it had without a word. In a tool for checking
                layouts, geometry quietly absent is the worst thing that can happen.

                Not dismissable, unlike the download notice. That one is about something that has finished;
                this is about what is on screen right now, and it stops being true only when another file
                is opened.
            *@
            @if (layoutNotice().Length > 0)
            {
                <div id="layoutNotice" class="row inlineNotice">
                    <div class="col">@layoutNotice()</div>
                </div>
            }

            @*The two file lists used to be here, as dialogs. They hang off their own buttons in the bar now.*@

            <!-- ***************************************************************************************************************** -->


            <!-- *************************************************** View ******************************************************** -->

            @*
                The files open, a tab each, over the view they take turns in.

                **Only once there are two.** A strip holding one tab says nothing the file's name does not, and it
                would push the drawing down for everybody who opens one file at a time - which is how a link or an
                embed still opens the app.
            *@
            @if (tabs.Count > 1)
            {
                <div id="fileTabs" class="row fileTabsRow">
                    <div class="fileTabs" role="tablist" aria-label="Open files">
                        @for (int i = 0; i < tabs.Count; i++)
                        {
                            int index = i;
                            bool onScreen = index == activeTab;

                            <div class="fileTab @(onScreen ? "fileTabOn" : "")" role="tab" aria-selected="@(onScreen ? "true" : "false")"
                                 title="@tabTitle(index)" @onclick="() => switchTab(index)">
                                @*
                                    Renamed where it is named, by a double-click: the name in a box, the extension after it
                                    and not in it, since what the file is does not change with what it is called. See
                                    startRenamingTab. Asked for 2026-10-31.
                                *@
                                @if (onScreen && renamingTab)
                                {
                                    <span class="fileTabRename" @onclick:stopPropagation="true">
                                        <input id="fileTabRename" class="fileTabRenameBox @(tabRenameProblem.Length > 0 ? "fileTabRenameBad" : "")"
                                               aria-label="File name" title="@tabRenameTitle()" spellcheck="false" @ref="tabRenameBox"
                                               style="width: @(Math.Max(3, tabRenameText.Length + 1))ch"
                                               @bind="tabRenameText" @bind:event="oninput" @onkeydown="tabRenameKey" @onkeydown:stopPropagation="true"
                                               @onblur="tabRenameLeft" />
                                        <span class="fileTabExtension">@CurrentlySelectedFileType</span>
                                    </span>
                                }
                                else
                                {
                                    <span class="fileTabName" @ondblclick="() => startRenamingTab(index)">@tabLabel(index)</span>
                                }

                                <button type="button" class="fileTabClose" aria-label="@($"Close {tabLabel(index)}")"
                                        title="Close this tab. A file you changed or brought in stays in the history list"
                                        @onclick="() => closeTab(index)" @onclick:stopPropagation="true">×</button>
                            </div>
                        }
                    </div>
                </div>
            }

            <div id="viewContainer" class="row">
    
                <!-- ************************************************** View ********************************************************* -->

                @*
                    height: inherit is gone - the row is a flex line now, so this stretches to it on its own.

                    Named, and told whether it takes a dropped file, for js/fileDrop.js. Both belong on this
                    box rather than on a view inside it: a file may be dropped in whichever view is up, and
                    this is the one element all three are mounted into. The attribute carries noEditing
                    rather than the script reading it off the disabled Open control, because what the script
                    needs to know is whether this page opens files at all - which is a thing the page can
                    say plainly, not a thing to infer from the state of a button somewhere else.
                *@
                <div id="viewPane" class="col viewBorderBackground viewPane" data-file-drop="@(noEditing ? "off" : "on")"
                     style="background-color: white; padding: 0; width: 80%;">
                            @switch (view)
                            {
                                case ViewType.ViewText: <TextEditor @ref="viewer" Gds="gds" CellListOpen="cellTreeOpen" OnCellListClosed="toggleCellTree" OnFileChanged="onFileRewritten" AutoPreview="autoPreview" OnAutoPreviewChanged="autoPreviewChosen"></TextEditor>;
                                    break;
                                case ViewType.View2DSvg: <Viewer2DSvg @ref="viewer" Gds="gds" Prepared="preparedLayout" Loading="loadInHand" Deck="drcDeck" ShowLayers="checkboxItems" DebugInfo="debugInfo" Markers="@(drcMarkers + reportMarkers + compareMarkers)" Notice="@drcNotice" NoticeIsFault="@drcNoticeIsFault" OnNoticeDismissed="dismissDrcNotice" History="editHistory" ReadOnly="noEditing" CellTreeOpen="cellTreeOpen" OnCellTreeClosed="toggleCellTree" OnSettingsChanged="saveSession" OnToolChanged="redrawToolbar" OnFileChanged="onFileEdited" OnDrawn="viewDrawn" AutoPreview="autoPreview" OnAutoPreviewChanged="autoPreviewChosen" OnLayerRenamed="renamed => applyLayerName(renamed.Key, renamed.Name)" Corner="linkChip"></Viewer2DSvg>;
                                    @*
                                        The rule that fades the locked layers, beside the view it applies to
                                        rather than inside it. Which layers are locked is the shell's
                                        question - the rows that say so are in the shell's own sidebar - and
                                        the view is handed a picture rather than asked to take a mode. See
                                        lockedStyle.
                                    *@
                                    @lockedStyle()
                                    break;
                                case ViewType.View3D: <Viewer3D @ref="viewer" Gds="gds" Loading="loadInHand" ShowLayers="checkboxItems" DebugInfo="debugInfo" FileName="@CurrentlySelectedFileName" LinkFile="@linkableFile()" CellTreeOpen="cellTreeOpen" OnCellTreeClosed="toggleCellTree" AutoPreview="autoPreview" OnAutoPreviewChanged="autoPreviewChosen" OnSettingsChanged="saveSession" OnToolChanged="redrawToolbar" OnDrawn="viewDrawn" Corner="linkChip"></Viewer3D>;
                                    break;

                                default:
                                    break;
                            }

                    @*
                        Centering, over the drawing rather than up in the bar.

                        It belongs to what is on screen, not to the page around it - the same reading that
                        puts Download Image on the canvas rather than beside the format picker. Full screen
                        and the banner switch stayed in the bar because those two really are about the page.

                        Here rather than inside either view, because both of them answer it and neither of
                        them owns the question: it is one button and one handler, and the pane is the box
                        both views are rendered into. Which also means it survives a view switch without
                        being written twice.

                        Top right, so it is the corner nothing else uses. The selection panel is top left,
                        and Download Image is bottom right - see .viewAction, whose look this shares and whose
                        corner it does not.

                        2D and 3D only. The text view is a list of records with no framing to put back, and
                        a button that did nothing on a third of the views would be worse than one that is
                        not there - see centerView for the same reasoning in C#.
                    *@
                    @*
                        What the last download could not carry, over the drawing rather than above it.

                        **The same box the drawing hint uses**, because it is the same kind of statement: a
                        line about what just happened, that appears and goes away. As a bar across the page
                        it pushed the whole view down to say something most files never need said, and the
                        layout jumped under the pointer at the moment somebody was reading about it.

                        Here in the pane rather than inside a view, for the reason the Center button below
                        gives: a download happens from the toolbar in every view, so a notice living in the
                        2D component would be a notice the 3D and text views could not show.

                        Above the view's own hint if they ever coincide. This is news and that is
                        reference - and only one of the two can be up at a time in there anyway, since the
                        drawing hint and the rule result share one chain.
                    *@
                    @*
                        What is being loaded, in the middle of the view, while it is - see loadingWhat. In the
                        pane rather than in a view for the reason the notice below gives: a file opens and a
                        view switches into whichever view is up, and the view arriving does not exist yet.

                        Always here, and empty while nothing loads, so the shell can draw it again on its own as a
                        load's file goes in - see fileWentIn.
                    *@
                    <LoadingNote @ref="loadingNote" What="@loadingWhat" Cancelable="@(loading is { CanCancel: true })"
                                 FileIn="loadFileIn" OnCancel="cancelLoad" />

                    @if (downloadNotice.Length > 0)
                    {
                        <div id="downloadNotice" class="drawHint viewNotice @(downloadRefused ? "drawHintError" : "")">
                            <span>@downloadNotice</span>

                            <button class="viewNoticeClose" @onclick="dismissDownloadNotice"
                                    aria-label="Dismiss this notice" title="Dismiss">&times;</button>
                        </div>
                    }

                    @*
                        What the rule check found, over the text - the same pill, words and id the 2D view puts in its
                        hint's place, which is drawn by that view and so is not here. Kept up until it is closed, as
                        there: a fault is gone to from the rules panel, and this is the part a line in the text
                        cannot say - how many there are, and whether every rule ran.
                    *@
                    @if (view == ViewType.ViewText && drcNotice.Length > 0)
                    {
                        <div id="drcNotice" class="drawHint viewNotice @(drcNoticeIsFault ? "drawHintFault" : "drawHintClear")">
                            <span>@drcNotice</span>

                            <button id="drcNoticeClose" class="viewNoticeClose" @onclick="dismissDrcNotice"
                                    aria-label="Close this message" title="Close">&times;</button>
                        </div>
                    }

                    @if (view == ViewType.View2DSvg || view == ViewType.View3D)
                    {
                        @*
                            Zooming by press, beside Center, for whoever is not on a wheel or a trackpad - and on
                            the same footing: one handler for both views, which each answer it their own way. Out,
                            then in, then Center, left to right.
                        *@
                        <button id="zoomOut" class="iconButton viewAction viewActionTop viewActionFarther" @onclick="() => zoomView(-ZoomPress)"
                                aria-label="Zoom out"
                                title="Zoom out">
                            <svg viewBox="0 0 16 16" aria-hidden="true">
                                <path d="M3 8h10" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.6" stroke-linecap="round" />
                            </svg>
                        </button>

                        <button id="zoomIn" class="iconButton viewAction viewActionTop viewActionBeside" @onclick="() => zoomView(ZoomPress)"
                                aria-label="Zoom in"
                                title="Zoom in">
                            <svg viewBox="0 0 16 16" aria-hidden="true">
                                <path d="M3 8h10M8 3v10" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.6" stroke-linecap="round" />
                            </svg>
                        </button>

                        <button id="centerView" class="iconButton viewAction viewActionTop" @onclick="centerView"
                                aria-label="Center the view"
                                title="Put the layout back in the middle of the window">
                            @*
                                Four arrows closing on a point, which is the thing this does. The crosshair
                                that was here first reads as *aiming* at something rather than gathering it
                                back, and at sixteen pixels its ticks were thin enough to be mistaken for
                                the full-screen corners two buttons along.
                            *@
                            <svg viewBox="0 0 16 16" aria-hidden="true">
                                <circle cx="8" cy="8" r="1.7" fill="currentColor" />
                                <path fill="currentColor" d="M5.9 1.3h4.2L8 4.4z" />
                                <path fill="currentColor" d="M5.9 14.7h4.2L8 11.6z" />
                                <path fill="currentColor" d="M1.3 5.9v4.2L4.4 8z" />
                                <path fill="currentColor" d="M14.7 5.9v4.2L11.6 8z" />
                            </svg>
                        </button>
                    }
                        </div>
    
                <!-- ***************************************************************************************************************** -->
    
                <!-- ************************************************** Layers ******************************************************* -->
                
                @*And the layer list goes with the bar, for the same reason: the canvas on its own.*@
                @if (!viewerOnly && layersOpen)
                {
                    <div id="layerSidebar" class="col-1 viewBorderBackground">

                        @*
                            The edge, to drag the list wider or narrower - see sidebars.js.

                            On the left, which is this panel's inner edge and the one a pointer reaches from
                            the drawing. Which is also why it grows the other way from the cell tree's: the
                            pointer moving right makes a left-hand panel wider and this one narrower.
                        *@
                        <div class="sidebarGrabber sidebarGrabberLeft"
                             data-sidebar-grab="layerSidebar"
                             data-sidebar-variable="--layer-sidebar"
                             data-sidebar-towards="left"
                             role="separator" aria-orientation="vertical"
                             title="Drag to resize"></div>


                        @*
                            The heading, and the way out of it.

                            A cross as well as the button in the bar, which is the pair the cell tree has:
                            the button is how it is got back, and this is where a hand already is when the
                            list is the thing in the way.
                        *@
                        <div class="sidebarTitle">
                            <div class="sidebarTitleMiddle">

                                @*
                                    Both names, and the one you are on is lit.

                                    **A switch that shows only the current state cannot say what it would do.**
                                    A single heading reading "Layers" had to carry arrows and a tooltip to hint
                                    that a second list existed at all, and somebody who never hovered it never
                                    found out. Two words side by side say there are two lists in the first
                                    glance, and pressing the unlit one is an obvious thing to try.

                                    Where a toolbar button for the rules used to be, and better than one: this
                                    sits on the panel it changes rather than in a bar across the page.

                                    Only where the rules can be checked: the 2D view, which draws a fault, and the
                                    text, which goes to its line - see rulesHere. The 3D view has nowhere to show one,
                                    so there it goes back to being a plain heading rather than a control that does
                                    nothing.
                                *@
                                @if (rulesHere())
                                {
                                    <div class="sidebarPanelPick" role="group" aria-label="Which list this panel shows">
                                        <button id="sidebarPanelLayers"
                                                class="sidebarPanelChoice @(sidebarShows == SidebarPanel.Layers ? "sidebarPanelChoiceOn" : "")"
                                                @onclick="() => showPanel(SidebarPanel.Layers)"
                                                aria-pressed="@(sidebarShows == SidebarPanel.Layers ? "true" : "false")"
                                                title="What is drawn, and how">
                                                <svg viewBox="0 0 16 16" width="14" height="14" aria-hidden="true">
                                                    <path d="M8 2 14.5 5.4 8 8.8 1.5 5.4Z" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4" stroke-linejoin="round" />
                                                    <path d="M1.5 8.3 8 11.7l6.5-3.4M1.5 11.2 8 14.6l6.5-3.4" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" />
                                                </svg>
                                                Layers
                                            </button>

                                        <button id="sidebarPanelRules"
                                                class="sidebarPanelChoice @(sidebarShows == SidebarPanel.Rules ? "sidebarPanelChoiceOn" : "")"
                                                @onclick="() => showPanel(SidebarPanel.Rules)"
                                                aria-pressed="@(sidebarShows == SidebarPanel.Rules ? "true" : "false")"
                                                title="What it is allowed to be, and whether it is">
                                                <svg viewBox="0 0 16 16" width="14" height="14" aria-hidden="true">
                                                    <path d="M3.5 1.8h6l3 3v9.4h-9Z" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4" stroke-linejoin="round" />
                                                    <path d="M9.5 1.8v3h3M5.6 8h4.8M5.6 10.3h4.8M5.6 12.6h3" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.3" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" />
                                                </svg>
                                                Rules
                                            </button>
                                    </div>
                                }
                                else
                                {
                                    <b>Layers</b>
                                }
                            </div>

                            @*
                                Named after whichever list is up, since the panel is now two of them.

                                **And never with "panel" in it.** A role-and-name lookup matches the name as
                                a substring, so "Close the panel" answers to a search for "Pan" - which is
                                the toolbar's pan tool, and three ruler tests started resolving to two
                                elements the moment this button was renamed. Nothing about the app was
                                wrong; the word simply contains the other one.
                            *@
                            <button id="layersClose" class="cellListClose"
                                    @onclick="toggleLayers"
                                    aria-label="@panelClose()"
                                    title="Close">&#128473;</button>
                        </div>

                        @if (sidebarShows == SidebarPanel.Rules)
                        {
                            @*
                                The rules, where the layers usually are.

                                **The same panel showing a different thing**, rather than a second panel: both
                                are a list down the side of the drawing, and they are never both wanted at once -
                                one says what is drawn and the other what it is allowed to be. What both list is
                                the PDK in use's, which the PDK manager over them is where to change.
                            *@

                            @*
                                A deck that is not the PDK's, and the way back to the PDK's rules.

                                **The one case the PDK manager cannot reach**: the deck that fails on purpose, from
                                the PDK manager's information on rules, or one imported before decks went there. A deck the
                                PDK did not put there stays for every file after it - see carryDeckOver - and Clear,
                                which used to take it off, went with the row it was in (2026-10-19). Said where the
                                row was, so a deck that fails everywhere is never mistaken for the PDK's.
                            *@
                            @if (drcDeck is not null && !deckFromPdk)
                            {
                                <div id="rulesNotPdk" class="rulesNotPdk">
                                    <span>@notPdkDeckSaid()</span>

                                    <button id="rulesBackToPdk" class="buttonStyle2 smallButton rulesButton" @onclick="backToPdkRules">@backToPdkLabel()</button>
                                </div>
                            }

                            @*
                                Check, and whether to keep checking.

                                **The switch is beside the button rather than in the settings**, because the
                                two are one question - when the layout gets measured - and reading the answer
                                should not mean opening a menu to find half of it.

                                **The button stays live with the switch on.** It went disabled, on the
                                argument that a control with nothing left to do should say so. But there is
                                always something left to do: a check runs on an *edit*, and plenty happens
                                that is not one - a deck imported, a cell flattened, or simply wanting the
                                marks back after reading them away. Taking the only manual run away because
                                an automatic one exists left no way to ask for the thing the panel is for.
                            *@
                            @if (drcDeck is not null)
                            {
                                <div class="rulesRun">
                                    <button id="drcRun" class="buttonStyle2 smallButton rulesButton rulesRunButton" @onclick="checkRules"
                                            title="@drcDeckDescription()">
                                        @*A tick in a circle, the check it runs (2026-11-01, asked for).*@
                                        <svg viewBox="0 0 16 16" width="14" height="14" aria-hidden="true">
                                            <circle cx="8" cy="8" r="6.3" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5" />
                                            <path d="M5.2 8.2 7.2 10.2 10.9 6.1" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.6" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" />
                                        </svg>
                                        DRC Check
                                    </button>

                                    <label class="rulesContinuous" title="@drcContinuousDescription()">
                                        <input type="checkbox" id="drcContinuous" checked="@drcContinuous" @onchange="toggleContinuous" />
                                        Check on every edit
                                    </label>

                                    @*
                                        What the run found, as the file KLayout's marker browser opens - the same
                                        report `gds drc -o` writes, with the faults waived here tagged the way
                                        KLayout tags them, so a waiver made here is a waiver there.
                                    *@
                                    @*A button like DRC Check's since 2026-11-01 (asked for), where it was a line of text with an icon.*@
                                    @if (drcResult is not null)
                                    {
                                        <button id="drcReport" class="buttonStyle2 smallButton rulesButton rulesRunButton" @onclick="exportRun"
                                                title="Download what the last check found as a KLayout report (.lyrdb), the faults waived here tagged waived">
                                            <svg viewBox="0 0 16 16" width="14" height="14" aria-hidden="true">
                                                <path d="M8 2.2v8.2M4.8 7.4 8 10.6l3.2-3.2M2.4 10.4v2.4a1 1 0 0 0 1 1h9.2a1 1 0 0 0 1-1v-2.4" fill="none" stroke="currentColor"
                                                      stroke-width="1.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" />
                                            </svg>
                                            Report
                                        </button>
                                    }
                                </div>

                                @*
                                    A check over part of the layout, said where its counts are read as well as over the
                                    view: the flatten stopped at its ceiling, or a cell contains itself, and every count
                                    in the list below is a count over what was there. See DrcResult.NotChecked.
                                *@
                                @if (drcResult is DrcResult lastRun && lastRun.NotChecked.Count > 0)
                                {
                                    <div id="drcNotChecked" class="rulesNotChecked">@string.Join(" ", lastRun.NotChecked)</div>
                                }
                            }

                            @*
                                The rules themselves, listed where the layers are listed.

                                Numbered like the layer rows, so the two panels read as the same shape. What each
                                row says is the rule's own id, what it measures and the limit it measures
                                against - which is what a deck is, one line at a time.
                            *@
                            <div class="layerList rulesList">
                                @pdkRow()

                                @*
                                    A KLayout report, above the deck: what another engine found, a category to
                                    a row.

                                    **Its own list rather than counts on the deck's rows.** A report's
                                    categories are whatever its script called them, and a deck here names its
                                    rules its own way - matching the two would be a guess, and a guess that
                                    put KLayout's count on the wrong row would be worse than two lists.

                                    **A row steps through its items**, which a deck's row does not: a run of
                                    this app's own is a press away, so its row only needs to say where to
                                    start, while a report is somebody else's list of things to go and look
                                    at, one after another - which is what KLayout's marker browser is for.
                                    Only the categories with something in them are listed; a report declares
                                    one for every rule its script ran, and most of a clean deck is empty.
                                *@
                                @if (report is not null)
                                {
                                    <div id="reportSection" class="rulesReport">
                                        <div class="listHeader rulesListHeader rulesReportHeader">
                                            <span class="listHeaderLabel rulesReportName" title="@reportTitle()">@reportName</span>

                                            <button type="button" id="reportClose" class="listHeaderRemove"
                                                    @onclick="forgetReportPressed"
                                                    title="Take this report and its markers off the drawing">×</button>
                                        </div>

                                        <div id="reportSummary" class="rulesReportSummary">@reportSummary()</div>

                                        @*What the check that wrote it did not get to, said the way a run of this app's own is - see DrcReportFile.NotChecked.*@
                                        @if (report.NotChecked.Count > 0)
                                        {
                                            <div id="reportNotChecked" class="rulesNotChecked">Not fully checked: @string.Join(" ", report.NotChecked)</div>
                                        }

                                        @{
                                            int shown = 0;
                                        }

                                        @foreach (var category in report.Categories)
                                        {
                                            if (category.Items == 0)
                                                continue;

                                            shown++;

                                            var name = category.Name;
                                            var hidden = reportHidden.Contains(name);

                                            <div class="rulesRow reportRow @(hidden ? "rulesRowOff" : "")"
                                                 title="@reportRowTitle(category)"
                                                 @onclick="() => stepReport(name)">
                                                <span class="rulesNumber">@(shown).</span>
                                                <span class="rulesId">@name</span>
                                                <span class="reportDescription">@category.Description</span>
                                                <span class="rulesCount">@reportCount(category)</span>

                                                @*The same eye a rule's row has, asking the same thing of the drawing.*@
                                                <button type="button"
                                                        class="rulesEyeButton @(hidden ? "rulesEyeOff" : "")"
                                                        @onclick="() => toggleReportCategory(name)"
                                                        @onclick:stopPropagation="true"
                                                        aria-pressed="@(hidden ? "false" : "true")"
                                                        title="@(hidden ? "Hidden. Click to draw this category's markers again" : "Drawn. Click to take this category's markers off the drawing")">
                                                    <svg viewBox="0 0 16 16" width="14" height="14" aria-hidden="true">
                                                        <path fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.3"
                                                              d="M1 8s2.5-4.2 7-4.2S15 8 15 8s-2.5 4.2-7 4.2S1 8 1 8Z" />
                                                        <circle cx="8" cy="8" r="1.9" fill="currentColor" />

                                                        @if (hidden)
                                                        {
                                                            <path stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.3" stroke-linecap="round" d="M2.5 13.5 13.5 2.5" />
                                                        }
                                                    </svg>
                                                </button>
                                            </div>
                                        }

                                        @if (reportAt >= 0 && reportAt < report.Items.Count)
                                        {
                                            <div id="reportCurrent" class="rulesReportCurrent">@reportItemSentence(report.Items[reportAt])</div>
                                        }
                                    </div>
                                }

                                @if (drcDeck is null)
                                {
                                    @*
                                        Nothing to check with, and why: the PDK in use checks nothing in this file, or
                                        has no rules yet. Either is the PDK manager's to change, which is over the
                                        panel - so this says which, rather than offering a file to load. See
                                        noRulesSaid.
                                    *@
                                    <div id="rulesNone" class="rulesEmpty">@noRulesSaid()</div>
                                }
                                else
                                {
                                    @*
                                        The whole deck at once, as the layer list has: one eye for every rule,
                                        and one × for all of them.

                                        **Back to normal first**, the same as there: with any rule switched off
                                        the eye switches every one on, and only with all of them on does it
                                        switch them all off. Removing every rule leaves the deck's layers and
                                        derivations in place: the deck stays, with its list of rules emptied.
                                    *@
                                    @if (drcDeck.Rules.Count > 0)
                                    {
                                        <div class="listHeader rulesListHeader">
                                            <span class="listHeaderLabel">All rules</span>

                                            <button type="button" id="allRulesOn"
                                                    class="listHeaderEye @(everyRuleOn() ? "" : "listHeaderEyeOff")"
                                                    @onclick="allRulesToggled"
                                                    aria-pressed="@columnState(drcDeck.Rules.Count(ruleIsOn), drcDeck.Rules.Count)"
                                                    title="@allRulesTitle()">
                                                <svg viewBox="0 0 16 16" width="14" height="14" aria-hidden="true">
                                                    <path fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.3"
                                                          d="M1 8s2.5-4.2 7-4.2S15 8 15 8s-2.5 4.2-7 4.2S1 8 1 8Z" />
                                                    <circle cx="8" cy="8" r="1.9" fill="currentColor" />

                                                    @if (!everyRuleOn())
                                                    {
                                                        <path stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.3" stroke-linecap="round" d="M2.5 13.5 13.5 2.5" />
                                                    }
                                                </svg>
                                            </button>

                                            @if (!noEditing)
                                            {
                                                @*Where a row's gear is, so the × below lines up with the column of them.*@
                                                <span class="listHeaderGap" aria-hidden="true"></span>

                                                <button type="button" id="removeAllRules" class="listHeaderRemove"
                                                        @onclick="removeAllRules"
                                                        title="Remove every rule from the deck">×</button>
                                            }
                                        </div>
                                    }

                                    @for (int i = 0; i < drcDeck.Rules.Count; i++)
                                    {
                                        var rule = drcDeck.Rules[i];

                                        @*
                                            A rule's row steps through the faults under it, a press at a time.

                                            Which is what a violation carrying its bounds is for. It went to the
                                            first one only, while there was nothing to do with a fault but look at
                                            it - the markers say where every one is. Now a fault can be waived, and
                                            waiving the third means getting to the third: the same walk a report's
                                            row takes, the line under the list saying which one is in front of you.
                                            A rule whose faults are all waived is not broken, and still steps.
                                        *@
                                        var broken = brokenRules.Contains(rule.Id);
                                        var found = drcSteps.ContainsKey(rule.Id);
                                        var id = rule.Id;
                                        var switching = rule;

                                        <div class="rulesRow @(broken ? "rulesRowBroken" : "") @(ruleIsOn(rule) ? "" : "rulesRowOff")"
                                             title="@ruleTitle(rule)"
                                             @onclick="() => stepRule(id)">
                                            <span class="rulesNumber">@(i + 1).</span>
                                            <span class="rulesId">@rule.Id</span>
                                            <span class="rulesCheckName">@rule.Check.ToString().ToLowerInvariant()</span>
                                            <span class="rulesLimit">@rule.Value</span>
                                            @if (found)
                                            {
                                                <span class="rulesCount @(broken ? "" : "rulesCountWaived")">@ruleCount(id)</span>
                                            }

                                            @*
                                                Whether this rule runs, as the eye a layer row uses - the same
                                                question, whether this is in the picture, about a rule.

                                                A view of the deck rather than an edit to it, so it is here in
                                                a deck nobody may change as well. Stopped from reaching the row,
                                                whose press goes to the rule's first fault.
                                            *@
                                            <button type="button"
                                                    class="rulesEyeButton @(ruleIsOn(rule) ? "" : "rulesEyeOff")"
                                                    @onclick="() => ruleToggled(switching)"
                                                    @onclick:stopPropagation="true"
                                                    aria-pressed="@(ruleIsOn(rule) ? "true" : "false")"
                                                    title="@(ruleIsOn(rule) ? "Checked. Click to leave this rule out of the check" : "Left out of the check. Click to check this rule again")">
                                                <svg viewBox="0 0 16 16" width="14" height="14" aria-hidden="true">
                                                    <path fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.3"
                                                          d="M1 8s2.5-4.2 7-4.2S15 8 15 8s-2.5 4.2-7 4.2S1 8 1 8Z" />
                                                    <circle cx="8" cy="8" r="1.9" fill="currentColor" />

                                                    @if (!ruleIsOn(rule))
                                                    {
                                                        <path stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.3" stroke-linecap="round" d="M2.5 13.5 13.5 2.5" />
                                                    }
                                                </svg>
                                            </button>

                                            @*
                                                What this rule is, behind one control - the same gear the
                                                layer rows carry, drawn the same way and in the same place,
                                                because it answers the same kind of question.

                                                **Stopped from reaching the row**, which is otherwise a press
                                                that jumps the view to this rule's first violation. Opening a
                                                rule to look at it is not a request to go and see where it
                                                failed, and a panel that did both would move the layout out
                                                from under whoever was reading it.
                                            *@
                                            @if (!noEditing)
                                            {
                                                var opening = rule;

                                                <button type="button"
                                                        class="rulesSettingsButton"
                                                        title="What this rule checks, and the line it is written on"
                                                        @onclick="e => openRuleSettings(opening, e)"
                                                        @onclick:stopPropagation="true">
                                                    <svg viewBox="0 0 16 16" width="13" height="13" aria-hidden="true">
                                                        <path fill="currentColor" d="M6.8 1h2.4l.3 1.9.9.5 1.7-.9 1.7 1.7-.9 1.7.5.9 1.9.3v2.4l-1.9.3-.5.9.9 1.7-1.7 1.7-1.7-.9-.9.5-.3 1.9H6.8l-.3-1.9-.9-.5-1.7.9-1.7-1.7.9-1.7-.5-.9L.7 9.2V6.8l1.9-.3.5-.9-.9-1.7 1.7-1.7 1.7.9.9-.5L6.8 1z" />
                                                        <circle cx="8" cy="8" r="2.4" fill="#fff" />
                                                    </svg>
                                                </button>
                                            }

                                            @*
                                                Taking the rule out, on its own row rather than in a menu.

                                                Stopped from reaching the row, or removing a rule would also
                                                be the click that jumps the view to its first violation -
                                                a fault in a rule that is on its way out.
                                            *@
                                            @if (!noEditing)
                                            {
                                                var going = rule;

                                                <button class="rulesRemove"
                                                        @onclick="() => removeRule(going)"
                                                        @onclick:stopPropagation="true"
                                                        title="Remove this rule from the deck">×</button>
                                            }
                                        </div>
                                    }

                                    @*
                                        A rule the deck holds and this build cannot measure is listed too, and
                                        listed as refused. Leaving it out would make the panel say the deck is
                                        smaller than it is.
                                    *@
                                    @foreach (string refused in drcDeck.Refused)
                                    {
                                        <div class="rulesRow rulesRowRefused" title="@refused">
                                            <span class="rulesId">@refused.Split(':')[0]</span>
                                            <span class="rulesRefusedWord">not measurable</span>
                                        </div>
                                    }

                                    @*
                                        The fault a row has stepped to, and what can be done about it here: waive it.

                                        **A waiver is a decision about one fault** - somebody has looked at it and
                                        let it stand, a pad's corner the deck's rule was never meant for - so it is
                                        made on the one in front of them rather than on a rule or an area. Here in
                                        a deck nobody may change too, like a rule's eye: it changes what is counted,
                                        not the layout or the deck.
                                    *@
                                    @if (currentFault() is DrcViolation fault)
                                    {
                                        <div id="drcCurrent" class="rulesReportCurrent rulesFault">
                                            <span class="rulesFaultSentence">@faultSentence(fault)</span>

                                            <button type="button" id="drcWaive" class="buttonStyle2 smallButton rulesButton"
                                                    @onclick="toggleWaiver"
                                                    title="@(waived.Contains(fault.Key) ? "Count this fault again" : "Mark this fault as looked at and accepted: drawn gray, left out of the count, and kept with the file while it stays exactly where it is")">@(waived.Contains(fault.Key) ? "Unwaive" : "Waive")</button>
                                        </div>
                                    }
                                }

                                @*
                                    A rule typed straight in, at the foot of the deck it joins - the same
                                    place, and for the same reason, as + Layer one panel over.

                                    One box in the deck's own grammar: the guide is a press away above, the
                                    parser already says exactly what is wrong with a line, and a form of
                                    dropdowns would either be most of a form builder or quietly unable to
                                    write half the decks people have.
                                *@
                                @if (drcDeck is not null && !noEditing)
                                {
                                    @if (addingRule)
                                    {
                                        <div class="addRuleRow">
                                            <input id="newRuleLine" type="text" class="addRuleBox"
                                                   placeholder="rule met1.w1 width met1 140 &quot;Metal 1 minimum width&quot;"
                                                   @bind="newRuleLine" @bind:event="oninput" @onkeydown="onAddRuleKey" />

                                            <button id="addRuleConfirm" class="buttonStyle2 smallButton rulesButton" @onclick="addRule">Add</button>
                                            <button id="addRuleCancel" class="buttonStyle2 smallButton rulesButton" @onclick="cancelAddingRule">Cancel</button>
                                        </div>

                                        @if (addRuleProblem.Length > 0)
                                        {
                                            <div id="addRuleProblem" class="addLayerProblem">@addRuleProblem</div>
                                        }
                                    }
                                    else
                                    {
                                        <div class="listAddRow">
                                            <button id="addRule" class="listAddButton" @onclick="startAddingRule"
                                                    title="Add one rule line to the deck, in the format the guide above describes">Add rule</button>
                                        </div>
                                    }
                                }
                            </div>
                        }
                        else
                        {
                            @*
                                Labels used to have one switch here, for the whole file. They are per layer now,
                                in each row's settings - which labels are worth reading depends on the layer, and
                                one switch could only ever be all of them or none.
                            *@

                            @*
                                A grid rather than bootstrap rows of fit-content columns, which is what left the
                                list ragged: every cell sized itself to its own contents, so the numbers, the
                                swatches and the checkboxes each sat at a different place on every line. Fixed
                                tracks line all four up, and the grid as a whole is centered in the sidebar.
                            *@
                            <div class="layerList">
                                @pdkRow()

                                @*
                                    The whole list at once, over the columns it acts on.

                                    **On the rows' own grid**, so the eye, the padlock and the × here sit
                                    exactly over the column of each below - which is what says they act on
                                    that whole column rather than on one layer. Held at the top while a long
                                    list scrolls under it.

                                    **Each switch goes back to normal first.** With any layer hidden the eye
                                    draws them all, and only with every one drawn does it hide them all; the
                                    padlock the same with locks. So the two moves people make are a press
                                    each: put everything away and open the one wanted, or undo a scatter of
                                    rows switched one by one. The glyph is the state the column is in, as it
                                    is on a row, so it says which way the next press goes.

                                    Its own classes rather than the rows', which are counted - a header
                                    answering to .layerEyeButton would be one more eye than there are layers.
                                *@
                                @if (checkboxItems.Count > 0)
                                {
                                    <div class="listHeader layerListHeader" title="@layerListSummary()">
                                        <span class="listHeaderLabel">All layers</span>

                                        <button type="button" id="allLayersShown"
                                                class="listHeaderEye @(everyLayerShown() ? "" : "listHeaderEyeOff")"
                                                @onclick="allLayersShownToggled"
                                                aria-pressed="@columnState(checkboxItems.Count(item => item.IsSelected), checkboxItems.Count)"
                                                title="@allLayersShownTitle()">
                                            <svg viewBox="0 0 16 16" width="14" height="14" aria-hidden="true">
                                                <path fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.3"
                                                      d="M1 8s2.5-4.2 7-4.2S15 8 15 8s-2.5 4.2-7 4.2S1 8 1 8Z" />
                                                <circle cx="8" cy="8" r="1.9" fill="currentColor" />

                                                @if (!everyLayerShown())
                                                {
                                                    <path stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.3" stroke-linecap="round" d="M2.5 13.5 13.5 2.5" />
                                                }
                                            </svg>
                                        </button>

                                        <button type="button" id="allLayersLocked"
                                                class="listHeaderLock @(anyLayerLocked() ? "listHeaderLockOn" : "")"
                                                @onclick="allLayersLockedToggled"
                                                aria-pressed="@columnState(checkboxItems.Count(item => item.IsLocked), checkboxItems.Count)"
                                                title="@allLayersLockedTitle()">
                                            <svg viewBox="0 0 16 16" width="14" height="14" aria-hidden="true">
                                                <rect x="3.2" y="7" width="9.6" height="6.6" rx="1.1"
                                                      fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.3" />

                                                @if (anyLayerLocked())
                                                {
                                                    <path fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.3" d="M5.6 7V5.2a2.4 2.4 0 0 1 4.8 0V7" />
                                                }
                                                else
                                                {
                                                    <path fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.3" d="M5.6 7V5.2a2.4 2.4 0 0 1 4.8 0" />
                                                }
                                            </svg>
                                        </button>

                                        @if (layersAreEditable)
                                        {
                                            <button type="button" id="removeAllLayers" class="listHeaderRemove"
                                                    @onclick="removeAllLayers"
                                                    title="Remove every layer, and every shape on them">×</button>
                                        }
                                    </div>
                                }

                                @{
                                    var chosenOn = chosenByLayer();
                                }

                                @for (int i = 0; i < checkboxItems.Count; i++)
                                {
                                        var item = checkboxItems[i];

                                        @*
                                            The row is a control while the Draw tool is out: clicking it
                                            takes that layer as the one a shape goes on, and the row is
                                            marked so the answer is where the layers are rather than only
                                            in a dropdown at the top of the screen. The rest of the time
                                            it is the list it always was.
                                        *@
                                        @*
                                            The same mark goes on every layer a chosen shape is on, so
                                            picking something out in the view says in the list which
                                            layer it is - the question the draw mark answers for the
                                            shape about to be made, asked of the one already there.
                                        *@
                                        @*
                                            The right button offers what can be done to the layer: name it,
                                            and copy or delete the shapes it holds in the cell being edited.

                                            **Drawn by the 2D view rather than here**, because two of the
                                            three are edits to the layout and this list is only a list -
                                            everything that acts on shapes lives in that component, and a
                                            second menu here would be a second set of the same three
                                            answers to keep in step. See Viewer2DSvg.OpenLayerMenu. In the
                                            3D and text views there is no such component and no menu, which
                                            is right: neither of them can act on a shape.
                                        *@
                                        <div class="layerRow @(layerRowsRespondToPress ? "layerRowPickable" : "") @(drawingOnLayer(item) ? "layerRowDrawing" : "") @(chosenOn.ContainsKey(item.Id) ? "layerRowChosen" : "") @(pulsingLayer == item.Id ? "layerRowPulsing" : "")"
                                             @onclick="() => layerRowPressed(item)"
                                             @oncontextmenu="e => openLayerRowMenu(item, e)"
                                             @oncontextmenu:preventDefault="true"
                                             title="@rowTitle(item, chosenOn)">
                                            <span class="layerIndex">@(i + 1).</span>

                                            @*
                                                The layer's color, where it is of use: next to the layer.
                                                It only reports - the gear at the end of the row is what
                                                opens the settings. A swatch that was itself a button meant
                                                the row had two things to press and no way to tell which
                                                did what, and the one that looked most like a control was
                                                the one that was really a readout.
                                            *@
                                            @*
                                                A pattern shows here too, or the list stops describing the
                                                drawing: two layers a shade apart are told apart in the view
                                                by their hatch and would be two identical swatches in the
                                                one place that exists to say which layer is which.

                                                An SVG only when there is one, so the ordinary case is the
                                                span it always was rather than eight elements per row.
                                            *@
                                            @if (fillOf(item) == LayerFill.None)
                                            {
                                                <span class="layerSwatch"
                                                      style="background-color: @colorOf(item.Id);"
                                                      title="@colorOf(item.Id)"></span>
                                            }
                                            else
                                            {
                                                <svg class="layerSwatch layerSwatchFilled" viewBox="0 0 16 16">
                                                    @*
                                                        A child element, not a title attribute: an inline
                                                        SVG is not an HTML element, and the attribute that
                                                        gives every span in this row its tooltip does
                                                        nothing here. The span above keeps using the
                                                        attribute because it is a span.
                                                    *@
                                                    <title>@colorOf(item.Id), @fillOf(item)</title>

                                                    @((MarkupString)SvgWriter.SwatchFor(fillOf(item), colorOf(item.Id), $"rowFill_{SvgWriter.ClassFor(item.Id)}", 16, patternColorOf(item), patternPixelsOf(item)))
                                                </svg>
                                            }

                                            @*
                                                The name, and only the name.

                                                **Renaming lives in the settings behind the gear**, which
                                                has had a box for it all along. This used to turn into a
                                                text box when it was clicked, and that made the row's own
                                                click mean two things at once: the draw tool wanted it to
                                                choose the layer to draw on, and draw-layer.spec had to
                                                pin which of the two won. A row that is a control has to
                                                be one control, and what it does now is choose the layer -
                                                see useDrawLayer.
                                            *@
                                            <span class="layerName">@item.Label</span>

                                            @*
                                                Whether this layer is drawn, as an eye.

                                                **A drawn eye rather than a checkbox**, because a
                                                checkbox says "on" and this says "shown" - and the row
                                                beside it now carries a second switch that is also on or
                                                off, and two identical boxes down a column say nothing
                                                about which is which. Open and struck through are two
                                                shapes, and the closed one is dimmed as well, so which
                                                layers are on screen can be read down the list rather
                                                than checked one row at a time.

                                                Stopped at the button, so a press on it is only what it
                                                is. Hiding a layer and taking it as the one to draw on in
                                                the same click are two answers to opposite questions.
                                            *@
                                            <button type="button"
                                                    class="layerEyeButton @(item.IsSelected ? "" : "layerEyeOff")"
                                                    @onclick="async () => await layerShownToggled(item)"
                                                    @onclick:stopPropagation="true"
                                                    aria-pressed="@(item.IsSelected ? "true" : "false")"
                                                    title="@(item.IsSelected ? "Drawn. Click to hide this layer" : "Hidden. Click to draw this layer")">
                                                <svg viewBox="0 0 16 16" width="14" height="14" aria-hidden="true">
                                                    <path fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.3"
                                                          d="M1 8s2.5-4.2 7-4.2S15 8 15 8s-2.5 4.2-7 4.2S1 8 1 8Z" />
                                                    <circle cx="8" cy="8" r="1.9" fill="currentColor" />

                                                    @if (!item.IsSelected)
                                                    {
                                                        <path stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.3" stroke-linecap="round" d="M2.5 13.5 13.5 2.5" />
                                                    }
                                                </svg>
                                            </button>

                                            @*
                                                Whether this layer can be worked on, as a padlock.

                                                **The third state the list was missing.** Hiding takes a
                                                layer off the screen, which is the wrong answer when it
                                                is the thing being worked *against*: a via has to line up
                                                with the metal over it, and nothing lines up with what it
                                                cannot see. A lock leaves it drawn and takes it out of
                                                reach - see CheckboxItem.IsLocked.

                                                Shackle up for open and down for shut, which is the one
                                                difference between the two glyphs, so the row reads at a
                                                glance without a color to carry it.
                                            *@
                                            <button type="button"
                                                    class="layerLockButton @(item.IsLocked ? "layerLockOn" : "")"
                                                    @onclick="async () => await layerLockToggled(item)"
                                                    @onclick:stopPropagation="true"
                                                    aria-pressed="@(item.IsLocked ? "true" : "false")"
                                                    title="@(item.IsLocked ? "Locked. Click to work on this layer again" : "Click to lock this layer: still drawn, but not selectable")">
                                                <svg viewBox="0 0 16 16" width="14" height="14" aria-hidden="true">
                                                    <rect x="3.2" y="7" width="9.6" height="6.6" rx="1.1"
                                                          fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.3" />

                                                    @if (item.IsLocked)
                                                    {
                                                        <path fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.3" d="M5.6 7V5.2a2.4 2.4 0 0 1 4.8 0V7" />
                                                    }
                                                    else
                                                    {
                                                        <path fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.3" d="M5.6 7V5.2a2.4 2.4 0 0 1 4.8 0" />
                                                    }
                                                </svg>
                                            </button>

                                            @*
                                                Everything about how this one layer is drawn, behind one
                                                control: its color and whether its labels are shown. Drawn
                                                as an inline SVG rather than an icon file, so it inherits
                                                the row's color and stays sharp at any zoom without another
                                                asset to vendor.

                                                **Gone in 3D**, with the rest of what this row can be made
                                                to do. A color is a property of the layer and lives in the
                                                layermap once it is changed, so setting one is an edit like
                                                any other - see layersAreEditable. What is left in that
                                                view is a press that flashes the slab and a box that hides
                                                it, neither of which changes anything.

                                                Lit while the popup is showing this layer, which says which
                                                row it belongs to now that pressing a row moves it there.
                                            *@
                                            @if (layersAreEditable)
                                            {
                                                <button type="button"
                                                        class="layerSettingsButton @(settingsOpenFor(item) ? "layerSettingsButtonOn" : "")"
                                                        aria-expanded="@(settingsOpenFor(item) ? "true" : "false")"
                                                        title="Color and labels for this layer"
                                                        @onclick="e => openLayerSettings(item, e)"
                                                        @onclick:stopPropagation="true">
                                                    <svg viewBox="0 0 16 16" width="13" height="13" aria-hidden="true">
                                                        <path fill="currentColor" d="M6.8 1h2.4l.3 1.9.9.5 1.7-.9 1.7 1.7-.9 1.7.5.9 1.9.3v2.4l-1.9.3-.5.9.9 1.7-1.7 1.7-1.7-.9-.9.5-.3 1.9H6.8l-.3-1.9-.9-.5-1.7.9-1.7-1.7.9-1.7-.5-.9L.7 9.2V6.8l1.9-.3.5-.9-.9-1.7 1.7-1.7 1.7.9.9-.5L6.8 1z" />
                                                        <circle cx="8" cy="8" r="2.4" fill="#fff" />
                                                    </svg>
                                                </button>
                                            }

                                            @*
                                                Taking the layer out, at the end of its own row.

                                                **Stopped from reaching the row**, which is otherwise a
                                                press that chooses this layer to draw on - so removing a
                                                layer would also be the click that picked it.
                                            *@
                                            @if (layersAreEditable)
                                            {
                                                var going = item;

                                                <button class="layerRemove"
                                                        @onclick="() => removeLayer(going)"
                                                        @onclick:stopPropagation="true"
                                                        title="Remove this layer, and any shapes on it">×</button>
                                            }
                                        </div>
                                }

                                @*
                                    **A list with nothing in it offers a PDK's layers**, which is where a new layout
                                    stands: its layers are read off its shapes, so it has none, and drawing needs a row
                                    to draw on. Set PDK opens the PDK manager, and the PDK used there lays its own set
                                    on, named, stacked and colored - see setPdk and startWithPdkLayers - or one at a time
                                    with Add layer below. Offered rather than laid on, since a new layout is
                                    deliberately named after nothing. It was a blue "Start with sky130's layers" until
                                    2026-10-31, asked to be a quiet button that asks which PDK.
                                *@
                                @if (layersWaitForPdk)
                                {
                                    <div id="layersEmpty" class="layerListEmpty">
                                        <span class="layerListEmptySays">No layers yet</span>

                                        <button id="layersSetPdk" class="layersSetPdk" @onclick="setPdk" title="@startPdkTitle()">Set PDK</button>
                                    </div>
                                }

                                @*
                                    A layer nothing is drawn on yet, at the foot of the list it joins.

                                    **In the card rather than over it.** What acts on the mapping as a whole is
                                    the PDK manager's; this puts one row in a list, so it belongs at the end of
                                    that list where the row will appear - the same place every list that can be
                                    added to puts its add.

                                    **Beside the text too** (2026-11-01, asked for) - see layersCanBeAdded.
                                *@
                                @if (gds is not null && layersCanBeAdded)
                                {
                                    @if (addingLayer)
                                    {
                                        <div class="addLayerRow">
                                            <input id="newLayerNumber" type="number" min="0" class="addLayerBox" placeholder="layer"
                                                   @bind="newLayerNumber" @bind:event="oninput" @onkeydown="onAddLayerKey" />
                                            <span class="addLayerSlash">/</span>
                                            <input id="newLayerDataType" type="number" min="0" class="addLayerBox" placeholder="type"
                                                   @bind="newLayerDataType" @bind:event="oninput" @onkeydown="onAddLayerKey" />

                                            <button id="addLayerConfirm" class="buttonStyle2 smallButton rulesButton" @onclick="addLayer">Add</button>
                                            <button id="addLayerCancel" class="buttonStyle2 smallButton rulesButton" @onclick="cancelAddingLayer">Cancel</button>
                                        </div>

                                        @if (addLayerProblem.Length > 0)
                                        {
                                            <div id="addLayerProblem" class="addLayerProblem">@addLayerProblem</div>
                                        }
                                    }
                                    else
                                    {
                                        <div class="listAddRow">
                                            <button id="addLayer" class="listAddButton" @onclick="startAddingLayer"
                                                    title="Add a layer to draw on. The format records a layer only through the shapes on it, so an empty one is gone when the file is written">Add layer</button>
                                        </div>
                                    }
                                }
                            </div>
                        }

                        @*
                            How see-through the shapes are drawn, under whichever list is up.

                            Outside the Layers/Rules switch rather than inside it: opacity is about the drawing,
                            and the drawing is there whichever list somebody is reading. Inside the layer branch it
                            disappeared the moment they pressed Rules - which is exactly when a marker over a stack
                            of half-transparent shapes is hardest to see.

                            It was an overlay across the top of the view, sitting on top of the very thing it
                            dims - a control you had to look past to see its own effect. It belongs with the
                            list of what it is dimming.

                            Only for the 2D view, which is the only one that has an opacity; the 3D view
                            stacks its layers in space instead. Reached by a cast rather than through
                            IRenderable, for the same reason the draw layer is: it is not a question every
                            view can answer.
                        *@
                        @if (viewer is Viewer2DSvg shaded)
                        {
                            <div class="layerOpacity">
                                <label for="layerOpacity">Opacity</label>

                                <input id="layerOpacity" type="range" min="0" max="1" step="0.1"
                                       value="@SvgWriter.FormatOpacity(shaded.Opacity)"
                                       @oninput="shaded.AdjustOpacity"
                                       title="How see-through the shapes are drawn, so you can look through stacked layers" />
                            </div>
                        }
                        else if (viewer is Viewer3D stacked)
                        {
                            @*
                                The 3D view's answer to the same question, in the same place.

                                Opacity and spacing are the two ways of seeing past the layer on top, one per
                                view - so whichever view is open, the control for it is the row under the
                                layer list rather than something floating over the drawing in one view and
                                not the other. It was an overlay across the bottom left of the scene, where
                                it sat over the geometry and took drags the orbit controls wanted.
                            *@
                            <div class="layerSpacing">
                                <label for="layerSpacing">Distance</label>

                                @*
                                    **From zero**, which is the process stack and nothing added to it.
                                    The minimum was 50 because the number used to be measured from the even
                                    stack's rung height rather than from zero - see DefaultLayerSpread.

                                    The maximum before the value: a browser clamps a range's value to the
                                    maximum it has at that moment, so a reach and a distance raised in the same
                                    render would leave the thumb at the old end if the value went on first.
                                *@
                                <input id="layerSpacing" type="range" min="0" max="@stacked.SpacingRange"
                                       value="@stacked.LayerSpacing"
                                       @oninput="stacked.AdjustLayerSpacing"
                                       title="How far apart the layers are stacked, to pull the layout open and see between them" />

                                @*
                                    How far the slider reaches, for a design that its old fixed end of 700 does
                                    not pull far enough apart. Bound @onchange - see AdjustSpacingRange.
                                *@
                                <input id="layerSpacingRange" class="spacingRange" type="number"
                                       min="@Viewer3D.LeastSpacingRange" max="@Viewer3D.MostSpacingRange" step="100"
                                       value="@stacked.SpacingRange"
                                       @onchange="stacked.AdjustSpacingRange"
                                       aria-label="How far the Distance slider reaches"
                                       title="How far the Distance slider reaches. Raise it to pull a big design with many layers further apart" />
                            </div>
                        }

                    </div>
                }

                <!-- ***************************************************************************************************************** -->
    
            </div>

            <!-- ***************************************************************************************************************** -->
        </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ************************************************************************************************************** -->

    @*
        What to do with a file opened while another one is already open.

        A modal rather than a bar notice, because nothing should be drawn or thrown away until it is
        answered - the two answers lead to different files being on screen, and a notice that can be ignored
        would leave the app having to pick one anyway.

        What the incoming file holds is on the panel because it is what the answer turns on: a file of one
        cell reads differently from a file of forty, and a name clash or a units mismatch is exactly the kind
        of thing that would otherwise be discovered later, in the drawing, as something looking wrong.
    *@
    @if (importPlan is not null)
    {
        <div id="importBackdrop" class="importBackdrop" @onclick="cancelImport"></div>

        <div id="importDialog" class="popupDiv popupZIndex_0 importDialog" @onclick:stopPropagation="true">
            <h5 class="popupTitle">@importName</h5>

            <p class="importSummary">
                @describeImport()
            </p>

            @if (importPlan.Renamed.Count > 0)
            {
                @*
                    Said before it happens rather than discovered afterwards in the cell list. A renamed cell
                    is the file's own name being taken by the layout that was open first, which is nobody's
                    mistake and still surprising to meet in a tree.
                *@
                <p class="importNote">
                    @renamedNote()
                </p>
            }

            @if (importPlan.Scale != 1)
            {
                <p class="importNote">
                    The two files measure in different units, so what comes in is resized to match this one.
                </p>
            }

            <div class="importButtons">
                <button id="importAsCell" class="buttonStyle2 importChoice" @onclick="importAsCell"
                        title="@($"Put its cells in this library and carry {importPlan.TopCell} on the pointer")">
                    Add to this layout
                </button>

                <button id="importAsFile" class="buttonStyle2 importChoice" @onclick="openImportedOnItsOwn"
                        title="Close what is open and show this file instead">
                    Open on its own
                </button>

                <button id="importCancel" class="buttonStyle2 importChoice" @onclick="cancelImport"
                        title="Keep what is open and forget this file">
                    Cancel
                </button>
            </div>
        </div>
    }

    <!-- ************************************************************************************************************** -->

    @*
        One rule's settings, laid out like one layer's and opened from the same gear.

        **It edits the rule's line, not a form of its parts.** A rule is a line of the deck - that is what is
        parsed, saved, exported and read back, which is what addRule's own note is about - and a DrcRule
        carries more than the four things a form would show it: an except clause, a window, a step, a metric,
        each present on some rules and not others. Composing a line back from boxes would quietly drop
        whichever of those this rule happened to use, and the rule would go on being listed while checking
        something narrower than it says. The line is lossless because it is the thing itself.

        So the boxes above it are a readout of what the parser made of that line, and the line is what can be
        typed. Applying re-reads the whole deck exactly as adding does, and refuses on the same terms.
    *@
    @if (ruleSettingsFor is not null)
    {
        <div class="popupDiv popupZIndex_0 ruleSettingsPopup"
             style="left: @(ruleSettingsLeft.ToString(CultureInfo.InvariantCulture))px; top: @(ruleSettingsTop.ToString(CultureInfo.InvariantCulture))px; max-height: calc(100vh - @(ruleSettingsTop.ToString(CultureInfo.InvariantCulture))px - 12px);">
            <div class="layerSettingsHeader">
                <span class="layerSettingsTitle">Rule settings</span>

                <button type="button" class="buttonStyle2 closeButton" @onclick="closeRuleSettings"
                        title="Close. Nothing is applied until Apply is pressed">&times;</button>
            </div>

            <hr class="layerSettingsRule" />

            @*
                What the deck currently makes of this rule. Read-only, because every one of them is decided
                by the line below - showing them as boxes would be offering to change a thing that is really
                changed somewhere else.
            *@
            <label class="layerSettingsRow" title="What this rule is called in the deck">
                <span>Name:</span>
                <span class="ruleSettingsValue">@ruleSettingsFor.Id</span>
            </label>

            <label class="layerSettingsRow" title="What this rule measures">
                <span>Check:</span>
                <span class="ruleSettingsValue">@ruleSettingsFor.Check.ToString().ToLowerInvariant()</span>
            </label>

            <label class="layerSettingsRow" title="The layers this rule is measured on">
                <span>Layers:</span>
                <span class="ruleSettingsValue">@string.Join(", ", ruleSettingsFor.Operands)</span>
            </label>

            <label class="layerSettingsRow" title="The limit this rule holds the layout to, in database units">
                <span>Limit:</span>
                <span class="ruleSettingsValue">@ruleSettingsFor.Value</span>
            </label>

            <hr class="layerSettingsRule" />

            @*
                The line itself, which is the only thing here that can be typed into.

                A textarea rather than an input: a rule with an except clause and a metric runs well past the
                width of this popup, and a line that has to be scrolled sideways to be read is one that gets
                edited blind.
            *@
            <label class="layerSettingsRow ruleSettingsLineRow" title="This rule as the deck holds it. The guide under the list says what one looks like">
                <span>Line:</span>
            </label>

            <textarea class="ruleSettingsLine"
                      rows="2"
                      @bind="ruleSettingsLine"
                      @bind:event="oninput"></textarea>

            @if (ruleSettingsProblem.Length > 0)
            {
                <div class="addLayerProblem ruleSettingsProblem">@ruleSettingsProblem</div>
            }

            <div class="ruleSettingsActions">
                <button type="button" class="buttonStyle2 smallButton rulesButton" @onclick="commitRuleSettings">Apply</button>
                <button type="button" class="buttonStyle2 smallButton rulesButton" @onclick="closeRuleSettings">Cancel</button>
            </div>
        </div>
    }

    <!-- ************************************************************************************************************** -->

    @*
        One layer's settings, as a popup like the About and QR ones rather than something that grows out of
        the sidebar. In the sidebar it pushed the layer list around as it opened and closed, and it had only
        the sidebar's width to lay a row of history swatches out in.

        It began as a color picker and holds everything about how one layer is drawn now - its color and
        whether its labels are shown.
    *@
    @if (settingsFor is not null || pdkSettings is not null)
    {
        @*
            The height it may take is measured from where it opens, not from the top of the window - it is
            fixed at the pointer and grows downwards, so a cap in the stylesheet alone bounds nothing. Without
            this the bottom rows fall off the screen on a short window with no way to reach them, which is
            what a Role row did the day it was added.
        *@
        <div class="popupDiv popupZIndex_0 layerSettingsPopup"
             style="left: @(pickerLeft.ToString(CultureInfo.InvariantCulture))px; top: @(pickerTop.ToString(CultureInfo.InvariantCulture))px; max-height: calc(100vh - @(pickerTop.ToString(CultureInfo.InvariantCulture))px - 12px);">
            @*
                The title is the name box. A layer's name is the one thing about it worth typing, and it
                was only reachable by clicking the row's own text in a sidebar narrow enough that the box
                had no room - here it has the width of the popup.

                It holds what the layer is actually called, which for an unnamed one is its pair. That was
                a placeholder over an empty box, which reads as a field waiting to be filled rather than
                as the name it already has - and left nothing to select and type over.

                Typing the pair back, or emptying the box, is taken as no name rather than as naming a
                layer after itself. See commitSettingsName.
            *@
            @*
                A title, because the panel had none.

                It opens over the layout with a name box, two numbers, a role and a color field in it, and
                nothing saying what any of that belongs to. The row it is opened from is a layer, and once
                the popup is up that row is behind it - so the one word that was missing is what this is.

                Left, with the close button still at the far end, which is what the header was laid out for
                before the name moved down to a line of its own.
            *@
            <div class="layerSettingsHeader">
                <span class="layerSettingsTitle">Layer settings</span>

                @*Opened on a PDK's row from the PDK manager, which is what it changes rather than the open file.*@
                @if (pdkSettings is not null)
                {
                    <span id="layerSettingsPdk" class="layerSettingsWhere">in @pdkSettings.Pdk.Name</span>
                }

                <button type="button" class="buttonStyle2 closeButton" @onclick="closeLayerSettings"
                        title="Close. Anything changed here has already been applied">&times;</button>
            </div>

            @*Closes the header off the way the footer closes the body, so the two rules bracket it.*@
            <hr class="layerSettingsRule" />

            @*
                Named like the switch below it, and laid out like it: what this layer is called and whether
                its labels are drawn are the two things here that are not the color, so they read as a pair
                of rows rather than as a field and then a setting.
            *@
            <label class="layerSettingsRow" title="What this layer is called. Clearing it, or typing @settingsKey() back, leaves it unnamed">
                <span>Name:</span>
                <input type="text"
                       class="layerSettingsName"
                       value="@settingsName"
                       @oninput="onSettingsNameInput"
                       @onkeydown="onSettingsNameKey"
                       @onblur="commitSettingsName" />
            </label>

            @*The open file's alone: a layermap has no column for whether a layer's labels are drawn.*@
            @if (settingsFor is not null)
            {
                <label class="layerSettingsRow" title="Draw the TEXT elements that sit on this layer">
                    <span>Show Labels:</span>
                    <input type="checkbox" class="layerLabelsToggle" checked="@settingsFor.ShowLabels" @onchange="labelsToggled" />
                </label>
            }

            @*
                No rule between this and the two boxes below.

                Name, labels, height and thickness are one run of settings about the layer itself, and a
                line through the middle of them said they were two groups. The rules that are left bracket
                that whole run off from the color picker under it.
            *@

            @*
                Where this layer sits in the wafer, and how thick it is.

                The 3D view stacks layers evenly until it is told otherwise, which is a guess that says
                only what order they are in. A process gives each layer a real height and thickness, and
                typing those here is what turns the picture from evenly spaced planes into the shape of an
                actual stack - the same thing GDS3D takes a process definition file for.

                Both in database units, which for a file whose UNITS say a database unit is a nanometer -
                as every bundled example does - means a process table can be typed in as it stands.
            *@
            <label class="layerSettingsRow" title="How far up the stack this layer sits, in database units. Blank puts it back on the even spacing">
                <span>Height:</span>
                <input type="number" step="1"
                       class="layerSettingsStack layerSettingsHeight"
                       value="@stackValue(height: true)"
                       @onchange="e => stackTyped(e, height: true)" />
            </label>

            <label class="layerSettingsRow" title="How thick this layer is drawn, in database units">
                <span>Thickness:</span>
                <input type="number" step="1" min="1"
                       class="layerSettingsStack layerSettingsThickness"
                       value="@stackValue(height: false)"
                       @onchange="e => stackTyped(e, height: false)" />
            </label>

            @*
                What the layer is for, which is the one thing here that no PDK table already carries and the
                one thing tracing a net needs. A file says which numbers it uses and never which of them are
                metal, so somebody has to say - and this is where, since the alternative is editing a CSV.
            *@
            <label class="layerSettingsRow" title="Whether this layer carries a net along itself, joins the layers it overlaps, or takes no part. Needed to trace a net">
                <span>Role:</span>
                <select class="layerSettingsRole" value="@settingsRole()" @onchange="roleChosen">
                    <option value="@LayerRole.None">none</option>
                    <option value="@LayerRole.Conductor">conductor</option>
                    <option value="@LayerRole.Via">via</option>
                </select>
            </label>

            <hr class="layerSettingsRule" />

            @*
                What is drawn over the color, which is why it sits with the color rather than with the stack.

                **Because color runs out before layers do.** A palette is the hue wheel divided by however
                many layers a file has, and past a dozen the steps are smaller than the difference an
                overlapping stack of half-transparent shapes makes to any of them. A pattern is a second axis
                - the same green, hatched rather than solid - and it survives being printed in gray.

                As buttons rather than a dropdown, because these are pictures. A list of the words "dots",
                "grid", "diagonal" makes somebody read a name and imagine the result; eight tiles make it the
                thing it is. They are the real patterns too, drawn by the same code that fills the layer -
                see patternTile - so a swatch cannot come to disagree with what the choice does.
            *@
            <div class="layerSettingsRow layerSettingsFills" title="A pattern over this layer's color, so two layers of a similar shade are still two layers">
                <span>Pattern:</span>

                <div class="layerFillChoices" role="group" aria-label="Fill pattern">
                    @foreach (var choice in fillChoices())
                    {
                        <button type="button"
                                class="layerFillChoice @(settingsFill() == choice.Fill ? "layerFillChoiceOn" : "")"
                                data-fill="@choice.Fill"
                                aria-pressed="@(settingsFill() == choice.Fill ? "true" : "false")"
                                title="@choice.Name"
                                @onclick="() => fillChosen(choice.Fill)">
                            @*
                                slice, not the default meet: the tile is square and the button is not, so
                                fitting the whole viewBox inside leaves a bar of popup either side of every
                                swatch - which on Solid is a red stripe in a white box rather than a solid
                                square. Slice covers the button and crops, which is the right answer for a
                                texture and keeps a dot round where stretching would not.
                            *@
                            <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" preserveAspectRatio="xMidYMid slice" aria-hidden="true">
                                @((MarkupString)patternTile(choice.Fill))
                            </svg>
                        </button>
                    }
                </div>
            </div>

            @*
                How big a repeat of it is, in pixels on the screen.

                **In pixels rather than in the file's units**, because the view holds a pattern at a constant
                screen size as the zoom changes - so the number that means anything to somebody looking at
                the drawing is the one at that end. A tile in database units would be a different size on
                every file, and a different size again at every zoom.

                Only where there is a pattern to size. On a solid layer this is a box whose number changes
                nothing, which reads as the control being broken rather than as not applying.
            *@
            @if (settingsFill() != LayerFill.None)
            {
                @*
                    The unit is in the label rather than beside the box, unlike the grid pitch two panels
                    away: Height and Thickness above are the same shape of row and put theirs in the
                    tooltip, and a third arrangement in one popup is a popup that looks assembled.
                *@
                <label class="layerSettingsRow"
                       title="How big one repeat of the pattern is on screen, in pixels. It stays this size as you zoom in">
                    <span>Size (px):</span>

                    <input type="number"
                           id="patternSize"
                           class="layerSettingsStack"
                           min="@LayerNames.LeastPatternPixels"
                           max="@LayerNames.MostPatternPixels"
                           step="1"
                           value="@settingsPatternPixels()"
                           @onchange="patternSizeTyped" />
                </label>
            }

            <hr class="layerSettingsRule" />

            @*
                **The picture the 3D view draws this layer with**, where it shows textures - its own section, since it is
                the one thing in here about the other view. Chosen from a file and kept in this browser under the file's
                name, which is all a session and a layermap carry: a PDK's Export puts the picture beside its CSV in a
                zip, and its Import brings it back - see PdkPackage.

                **Choosing one turns the 3D view's Show textures on**, which is off until somebody turns it on - so the
                layer is seen with its picture the moment that view opens, rather than not at all.

                Its size is in the layout's own units, like the height and the thickness above, since a picture of a
                surface is a picture of something a size: a micron to begin with, in the file's own units.
            *@
            <div class="layerSettingsRow layerSettingsFills"
                 title="A picture tiled over this layer in the 3D view, where it shows textures. Kept in this browser, and in the layermap by its file name">
                <span>Texture for 3D view:</span>
            </div>

            @*
                The name over the two actions rather than beside them: the popup is as wide as its color square, and on
                one line with both the name had room for two letters of itself.
            *@
            <div class="layerTexture">
                @if (settingsTexture() is not null && textureShown.Length > 0)
                {
                    <img id="layerTextureThumb" class="layerTextureThumb" src="@textureShown" alt="" />
                }

                <div class="layerTextureSaid">
                    @if (settingsTexture() is string texture)
                    {
                        <span id="layerTextureName" class="layerTextureName" title="@texture">@texture</span>
                    }
                    else
                    {
                        <span id="layerTextureName" class="layerTextureName layerTextureNone">None</span>
                    }

                    <div class="layerTextureActions">
                        @*
                            The kinds pictureType knows, which are the kinds that need no type to be drawn: the store keeps the
                            bytes alone, and an SVG handed over without its type is no picture at all.
                        *@
                        <InputFile id="layerTextureFile" style="display: none;" accept="@TexturePictures" OnChange="textureChosen" />
                        <label for="layerTextureFile" class="layerSidebarAction" title="A PNG, JPEG, WebP, GIF or BMP from this computer, up to 16 MB">Choose image…</label>

                        @if (settingsTexture() is not null)
                        {
                            <span id="layerTextureRemove" class="layerSidebarAction" @onclick="removeTexture" title="Draw this layer in 3D without a picture again">Remove</span>
                        }
                    </div>
                </div>
            </div>

            @if (textureMissing)
            {
                <div id="layerTextureMissing" class="layerTextureMissing">
                    This picture is not kept in this browser, so the layer is drawn without it. Choose it again, or import the layermap with it.
                </div>
            }

            @if (settingsTexture() is not null)
            {
                <label class="layerSettingsRow" title="How big one repeat of the picture is on the layout, in database units. Blank is a micron">
                    <span>Texture size:</span>

                    <input type="number"
                           id="textureSize"
                           class="layerSettingsStack"
                           min="1"
                           step="1"
                           placeholder="@defaultTextureUnits()"
                           value="@settingsTextureUnits()"
                           @onchange="textureSizeTyped" />
                </label>
            }

            <hr class="layerSettingsRule" />

            @*
                Named, the way the pattern above it is.

                Everything higher up this popup is a labeled row - Name, Show Labels, Height, Thickness,
                Role, Pattern - and then the biggest control in it arrived with no word at all: a square of
                color, a slider and three numbered boxes, left to be recognized. It is recognizable, and it
                was still the one section a reader had to identify rather than read.
            *@
            @*
                And which of the layer's two colors it changes.

                **One picker, aimed.** A layer with a pattern has a color for the ground and a color for the
                marks over it, and a second square-slider-and-three-boxes for the second one would double the
                tallest section of the tallest popup in the app. The switch says which of them the control
                below is currently about, which is a row rather than a second control.

                Only where there are two. A solid layer has one color, and a toggle offering to change the
                other one is a control with nothing on the end of it.
            *@
            <div class="layerSettingsRow layerSettingsFills">
                <span>Color:</span>

                @if (canColorPattern())
                {
                    <div class="colorTargets" role="group" aria-label="Which color to change">
                        <button type="button"
                                id="colorBackground"
                                class="colorTarget @(colorTarget == ColorTarget.Background ? "colorTargetOn" : "")"
                                aria-pressed="@(colorTarget == ColorTarget.Background ? "true" : "false")"
                                title="Change the color the layer is filled with"
                                @onclick="() => aimColorAt(ColorTarget.Background)">Background</button>

                        <button type="button"
                                id="colorPattern"
                                class="colorTarget @(colorTarget == ColorTarget.Pattern ? "colorTargetOn" : "")"
                                aria-pressed="@(colorTarget == ColorTarget.Pattern ? "true" : "false")"
                                title="Change the color the pattern's marks are drawn in"
                                @onclick="() => aimColorAt(ColorTarget.Pattern)">Pattern</button>
                    </div>
                }
            </div>

            @*
                The picker itself, open, rather than a swatch that opens the operating system's one over
                the top of this. A native dialog covers the layout, which is the thing being recolored -
                and the whole reason this popup sits over the drawing instead of in the sidebar.

                Saturation across, value down, hue on the slider. The field's own background is the hue at
                full strength; the two gradients over it wash it out to white going left and down to black
                going down, which is the standard square and costs no images.
            *@
            @*
                mouseup and change are what put a color in the history, not the dragging - see
                colorSettled. mouseup sits on the field rather than the popup because a release outside
                the field is a drag that wandered off, and the color under the pointer when it left is
                not one anybody chose.
            *@
            <div class="layerSettingsField"
                 style="background-color: @hueColor();"
                 @onmousedown="fieldPressed"
                 @onmousemove="fieldDragged"
                 @onmouseup="colorSettled"
                 title="Drag to choose. Across for how strong the color is, down for how dark">
                @*
                    pointer-events are off it in the CSS. Without that, a press that lands on the marker
                    reports its offset within the marker rather than within the field, and the color jumps
                    to a corner the moment you try to nudge the one you already have.
                *@
                <span class="layerSettingsFieldMark"
                      style="left: @percent(pickedColor.Saturation); top: @percent(1 - pickedColor.Value);"></span>
            </div>

            @*oninput draws as it slides, onchange is the one that lands - so only the latter is kept.*@
            <input type="range" class="layerSettingsHue" min="0" max="360" step="1"
                   value="@pickedColor.Hue.ToString("F0", CultureInfo.InvariantCulture)"
                   @oninput="hueChanged"
                   @onchange="colorSettled"
                   title="The hue, all the way round the wheel" />

            @*
                The same color as numbers, for when it is a number you have rather than a shade you are
                looking for - a value out of a layer properties file, or one being matched to another
                tool's.
            *@
            <div class="layerSettingsChannels">
                @foreach (var channel in rgbChannels())
                {
                    <label title="@channel.Name, 0 to 255">
                        <span>@channel.Letter</span>
                        <input type="number" min="0" max="255" step="1"
                               class="layerSettingsChannel"
                               value="@channel.Level"
                               @onchange="e => channelTyped(e, channel.Letter)" />
                    </label>
                }
            </div>

            @*
                The colors picked before this one, newest first, so a scheme can be applied across several
                layers without hunting the same shade out of a gradient each time.
            *@
            @if (recentColors.Count > 0)
            {
                <div class="layerSettingsHistoryTitle" title="Colors used recently, newest first. Click one to give this layer the same color">History</div>
                <div class="layerSettingsHistory">
                    @foreach (string recent in recentColors)
                    {
                        <button type="button"
                                class="layerSwatch"
                                style="background-color: @recent;"
                                title="Give this layer @recent"
                                @onclick="() => chooseRecentColor(recent)"></button>
                    }
                </div>
            }

            <div class="layerSettingsFooter">
                @*
                    What "put it back" means depends on which color the picker is on: the layer's own goes
                    back to its place on the gradient, and the pattern's goes back to following the layer.
                    One action rather than two, because only one of them is reachable at a time.
                *@
                @if (colorTarget == ColorTarget.Pattern)
                {
                    <span id="resetColor" class="layerSidebarAction" @onclick="resetColor"
                          title="Draw the pattern in the layer's own color again">Match the layer</span>
                }
                else
                {
                    <span id="resetColor" class="layerSidebarAction" @onclick="resetColor"
                          title="Give this layer back the color it was assigned from the gradient">Reset to palette</span>
                }

                @*Only when there is something to put back, the same way Clear History only appears with rows to clear.*@
                @if (stackIsCustom())
                {
                    <span class="layerSidebarAction" @onclick="resetStack" title="Put this layer back on the even spacing the view works out for itself">Reset stack</span>
                }

                @*
                    Removing the layer is the × at the end of its row, not a line in here.

                    It was both for a while, which is one thing said in two places - and the row is the
                    better of the two: it is where the layer is, it is one press rather than two, and every
                    other list in the app that can be shortened is shortened from its own rows.
                *@
            </div>
        </div>
    }


    @*The share popup, over the whole page rather than over a view: a link or an embed is about all of it.*@
    @if (shareFrom is not null)
    {
        <ShareDialog BaseUri="@NavigationManager.BaseUri"
                     LinkableFile="@linkableFile()"
                     UnlinkableReason="@unlinkableReason()"
                     FileEdited="fileEdited"
                     View="@slugOf(view)"
                     Box="@shareFrom.Framing"
                     Camera="@shareFrom.Camera"
                     OnClose="closeShare" />
    }

    @*
        The assistant's two windows: the conversation, and the models it can use.

        **Mounted with the page and only hidden**, so a conversation outlives closing its window and switching view - it
        is about the file, which all three views show. Left out of a page that wants the drawing alone.
    *@
    @if (!viewerOnly)
    {
        <AskAi @ref="askAi"
               Visible="askAiOpen"
               Rules="ruleBook"
               Pdks="pdkShelf"
               OnClose="closeAskAi"
               Gds="gds"
               FileRewrites="fileRewrites"
               History="editHistory"
               FileName="@CurrentlySelectedFileName"
               ReadOnly="noEditing"
               Deck="drcDeck"
               RulesOff="rulesOff"
               Waived="waived"
               Describe="describeForAssistant"
               Look="lookForAssistant"
               Examples="assistantExamples"
               BeforeSend="saveTextFirst"
               OnLayoutChanged="changedOnHistory"
               OnOpenSettings="openAiSettings" />

        @*The models and the rest of the AI's settings, and the MCP server - not on a page that may not edit. See LiveLinkCard.*@
        <AiSettings Visible="aiSettingsOpen" OnClose="closeAiSettings" Link="@(noEditing ? null : liveLink)" LinkAsked="linkAsked" />

        @*The PDKs, each with its layers and rules - see PdkManager. The panels edit the one in use as well.*@
        <PdkManager Visible="pdkManagerOpen" OnClose="closePdkManager" Pdks="pdks" Picking="pickingPdk"
                    ShippedLayers="shippedLayers" ShippedRules="shippedRules"
                    RulesLive="deckFromPdk" RulesOff="rulesOff" OnRuleToggled="pdkRuleToggled"
                    OnUse="usePdk" OnEdited="pdkEdited" OnListChanged="pdksChanged" OnStyleLayer="openPdkLayerSettings" OnDemoDeck="loadStrictDeck"
                    OnWriteRules="writeRulesWithAi" OnWriteLayers="writeLayersWithAi" />

        @*
            In the 2D and 3D views the chip is drawn inside the drawing, above the pointer's position - see linkChip.
            The text view has no corner of its own for it, so there it keeps the window's.
        *@
        @if (view == ViewType.ViewText)
        {
            <LiveLinkChip Link="liveLink" Hidden="aiSettingsOpen" OnOpen="openLiveLink" />
        }

        @*The command palette, on Ctrl+K - the commands built only while it is open, since nothing else reads them.*@
        <CommandPalette Visible="paletteOpen" Commands="@(paletteOpen ? paletteCommands() : noCommands)" OnClose="closePalette" />

        <GuidedTour Visible="tourOpen" OnClose="closeTour" />

        <TourQuestion Visible="askTour" OnAnswer="answerTourQuestion" />
    }

    @*The space under the viewer is padding on .content now, rather than three empty paragraphs.*@

</div>

@code {

    #region Variables ****************************************

    private IRenderable? viewer = null;

    ///<summary>
    ///What has been edited into the open file and what can be taken back.
    ///
    ///**Here rather than in the view that makes them.** An undo stack belongs to the file: the 2D editor is
    ///destroyed and rebuilt every time somebody looks at the 3D view and comes back, and a stack that lived
    ///there went with it. It is also written into a session from here, which is what lets it outlive the
    ///page - see <see cref="restoreEdits"/>.
    ///
    ///Not to be confused with <c>history</c>, which is the list of files that have been opened.
    ///</summary>
    private readonly EditHistory editHistory = new EditHistory();

    ///<summary>
    ///The view whose toolbar the last render drew. @ref is only assigned once a render has completed, so
    ///the pass that switches views draws no toolbar and this is what asks for the one more pass needed.
    ///</summary>
    private IRenderable? toolbarRenderedFor = null;

    private ViewType view = ViewType.View2DSvg;

    ///<summary>
    ///Which view to show, from ?view= in the address. This is what makes a link - or the QR code, which
    ///exists to get you from a desktop onto a phone or a headset - land on the view it was taken from
    ///rather than always on the 2D one.
    ///</summary>
    [SupplyParameterFromQuery(Name = "view")]
    public string? ViewSlug { get; set; }

    ///<summary>
    ///Which bundled example to open, from ?file= in the address - the file name without its .gds. An
    ///uploaded file cannot be named here: it exists only in the tab it was dropped into.
    ///
    ///A way in rather than a description of what is open: it leaves the address once its file is open, and
    ///nothing writes it back. See takeOutOfAddress.
    ///</summary>
    [SupplyParameterFromQuery(Name = "file")]
    public string? FileSlug { get; set; }

    ///<summary>The example that ?file= has already been acted on for, so a re-render does not fetch it again.</summary>
    private string? openedFileSlug = null;

    ///<summary>
    ///Whether the app is inside somebody else's page, in an iframe, which makes the address theirs - see
    ///takeOutOfAddress. Read first thing on the first render: whatever takes a parameter out waits on the
    ///example list and then on a file's fetch, so this is in hand long before.
    ///</summary>
    private bool inIframe = false;

    ///<summary>
    ///Whether the example list has been fetched, which ?file= cannot be resolved without.
    ///
    ///It matters because OnInitializedAsync yields on that fetch, so Blazor renders once before it
    ///returns - and the first OnAfterRenderAsync therefore runs while the list is still empty. Without
    ///this, a linked example was reported as not existing.
    ///</summary>
    private bool exampleListFetched = false;

    ///<summary>
    ///What the picker shows. Bound rather than left to a hardcoded selected option so that arriving on a
    ///?file= link, or an upload clearing it, leaves the picker agreeing with what is loaded.
    ///</summary>
    private string selectedExample = "";
    public string CurrentlySelectedFileName { get; set; } = "";
    public string CurrentlySelectedFileType { get; set; } = "";
    private List<CheckboxItem> checkboxItems = new List<CheckboxItem>();

    ///<summary>
    ///Where the whole session lives. One key rather than one per setting, because the pieces only make
    ///sense together - a layer's visibility means nothing without the file that has that layer.
    ///</summary>
    private const string SessionStorageKey = "gdsviewer.session";

    ///<summary>
    ///Where the session's file bytes live when it carries them: beside it, written only when the file is
    ///written whole rather than with every save. See SavedSession.BytesApart and SavedSession.Journal.
    ///</summary>
    private const string SessionBytesKey = SessionStorageKey + HistoryStore.BytesSuffix;

    ///<summary>
    ///Which writing of which file the bytes beside the session are, or null when that is not known to be the
    ///file on screen - which makes the next save that carries bytes write them. See saveSession.
    ///</summary>
    private BytesBeside? sessionBytesHeld = null;

    ///<summary>
    ///Whether bytes might still be sitting beside the session from a file that carried them, so a save that
    ///carries none should clear them away. True to begin with, since a session from the last visit may have.
    ///</summary>
    private bool sessionBytesLeft = true;

    ///<summary>
    ///A restored session waiting for the view it belongs to. The view is created by the render after the
    ///file is opened, so its settings cannot be applied at the moment the session is read.
    ///</summary>
    private SavedSession? restoredSession = null;

    ///<summary>
    ///Tried once and only once. OnAfterRenderAsync runs on every render, and a session that failed to
    ///restore must not be retried on each of them.
    ///</summary>
    private bool sessionRestoreAttempted = false;

    //The shell had a handle here for the view menu's dismissal. The view switch is three buttons now, so
    //there is no menu to dismiss and nothing calling back - see the toolbar's own comment.

    ///<summary>
    ///Set while a saved state is being put back, so that nothing writes over it half-applied.
    ///
    ///Putting a state back is not one step. Restoring an example refetches it, and that load saves - at
    ///which point the layers are the defaults the load produced and the view has not been handed its
    ///controls yet, because the view is created by the render *after* this. So the save wrote defaults
    ///over the very session being read, and the state only survived because it was already in hand: the
    ///page looked right and the store no longer was, so the reopen after that one lost it.
    ///
    ///Cleared in <see cref="applyRestoredSettings"/>, which is the render where the last of it lands.
    ///</summary>
    private bool restoringState = false;

    ///
    ///The PDKs kept and which one is in use - see Models/Pdk.cs. Read at start, before anything can open, and saved
    ///whenever one changes: from the PDK manager, or from the layer and rule panels, which edit the PDK in use.
    ///
    ///**In place of the names that carried from file to file**, which was one PDK nobody could see, name or keep two of:
    ///a set of names taken off whatever file was open last. What carries now is the PDK in use, and it carries to a
    ///file drawn in it - see fileUnderPdk.
    ///
    private PdkList pdks = PdkList.Fresh();

    ///<summary>Whether the list was read from storage rather than made fresh - so what came before PDKs is carried in only once.</summary>
    private bool pdksKept;

    ///<summary>The names a session carried before there were PDKs, read at start for <see cref="carryIntoPdk"/>.</summary>
    private string carriedBeforePdks = "";

    ///
    ///Whether the open file is drawn in the PDK in use, so the PDK names its layers and checks it: most of its layers
    ///are the PDK's own, or it was put under it by hand - see pdkTaken. Worked out each time a file opens or comes
    ///back, and when the PDK in use changes.
    ///
    ///**Most of its layers, not the file's origin.** The bundled sky130 names were laid over the bundled examples
    ///only, since a file off somebody's machine could be any technology and sky130 labels on another process's layout
    ///read as right when they are wrong. That reason holds, and it is what the test is for: a sky130 upload is named
    ///now, and a file from another process keeps its numbers - see LayerNames.Fits.
    ///
    private bool fileUnderPdk;

    ///
    ///Whether the open file was put under the PDK by hand - a layout started from the PDK's layers, which Load in
    ///either panel did too until 2026-10-19 - which keeps it there however its layers fit. Kept in its session. See
    ///fileUnderPdk.
    ///
    private bool pdkTaken;

    ///<summary>Whether the deck loaded is the PDK's, laid over a file drawn in it, rather than one loaded by hand. See carryDeckOver.</summary>
    private bool deckFromPdk;

    ///<summary>
    ///Whether the Examples popup is up. Not saved into a session: it is a thing being done rather than a
    ///setting, and coming back to a dialog nobody left open would be a surprise.
    ///</summary>
    private bool showExamples = false;

    ///
    ///The picture in the frame beside the Examples and History lists - a PNG's or an SVG's data address, or empty - and whose it is.
    ///
    ///**Whose, apart from the row pointed at**, because a picture stays on show while the next one is drawn - see
    ///previewDrawing - and until that one is ready it is still a picture of the row before.
    ///
    private string previewPicture = "";
    private string previewShownFor = "";

    ///<summary>Whether the row pointed at is being drawn, which the frame shows once that has taken a moment.</summary>
    private bool previewDrawing = false;

    ///<summary>What the list is narrowed to, and the rows that survive it.</summary>
    private string exampleFilter = "";
    private List<ExampleRow> filteredExamples = new List<ExampleRow>();

    ///<summary>The injected files the built list holds. See injectedSignature.</summary>
    private string injectedInList = "";

    ///<summary>
    ///One line of the example list: either a file to open, or the heading over a group of them.
    ///
    ///Headings are rows rather than markup around groups because the list is virtualized - Virtualize
    ///renders a window of one flat sequence, so anything that has to appear in the right place has to be
    ///in that sequence. It also means a heading scrolls away with its group rather than hanging over the
    ///wrong one.
    ///</summary>
    private readonly record struct ExampleRow(string Name, bool IsHeading);

    private const string TestExamplesHeading = "Test GDS Files";
    private const string DesignExamplesHeading = "Sky130 Designs";
    private const string Sky130ExamplesHeading = "Sky130 GDS Examples";

    ///
    ///The embedder's own files, over the ones that ship with the app.
    ///
    ///First in the list, because a page that went to the trouble of naming its own files means those to be
    ///the ones on offer - and named for where they came from rather than "Examples", so somebody who did
    ///not build the page can tell whose files these are.
    ///
    private const string InjectedExamplesHeading = "From this page";

    ///<summary>Matches the CSS. Virtualize needs to know a row's height to know how many fit.</summary>
    private const float ExampleRowHeight = 22;

    ///<summary>
    ///The pictures drawn for both lists, so going back over a list a second time costs nothing - and the one being drawn,
    ///given up when the pointer settles on another row. See PreviewPictures.
    ///
    ///Capped, because hovering the length of the list would otherwise hold a picture of every bundled cell at once.
    ///</summary>
    private readonly PreviewPictures previewPictures = new PreviewPictures(MaximumCachedPreviews);
    private const int MaximumCachedPreviews = 40;

    ///<summary>
    ///The row the preview is currently for, and the wait that stops a sweep across the list fetching
    ///every file it passes over.
    ///</summary>
    private string previewFor = "";
    private CancellationTokenSource? previewWait = null;
    private const int PreviewHoverDelayMs = 160;

    ///<summary>
